**Taints and tolerations:**

    Taints=nodes
    tolerations=onpods


Both taints and tolerations in kubernetes for scheduling.

Example: Consider a simple cluster with three worker nodes (node one, two, and three) and four pods (A, B, C, and D). By default, without any taints or tolerations, the scheduler distributes the pods evenly across all nodes.
Suppose you want to dedicate node one to a specific application:
First, taint node one (using, for example, the key-value pair “app=blue”) so that no pods are scheduled there by default.
Then, add an appropriate toleration to the dedicated pod (Pod D) so that it alone can run on node one.

When the scheduler assigns pods:
Pod A, lacking a toleration, will be scheduled on node two.
Pod B is repelled from node one and lands on node three.
Pod C, without a matching toleration, is scheduled on node two or three.
Pod D, having the correct toleration, is scheduled on node one.

**Tainting a node:**

kubectl taints nodes node-name key=value:taint-effect

kubectl taint nodes node1 app=blue:NoSchedule

🔴 1. NoSchedule
👉 New pods will NOT be scheduled here
🎯 Use case:
You have a GPU node and you don’t want normal apps running there.

Step 1 — Taint the Node

kubectl taint nodes node1 gpu=true:NoSchedule
Meaning:
❌ "Don’t schedule any pod on this GPU node unless it has permission."

Step 2 — Normal Pod (Without toleration)

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: normal-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx
➡️ This pod WILL NOT be scheduled on node1

Step 3 — GPU Pod (With toleration)

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: gpu-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: cuda
    image: nvidia/cuda
  tolerations:
  - key: "gpu"
    value: "true"
    effect: "NoSchedule"
➡️ Now this pod CAN run on node1

🟡 2. PreferNoSchedule
👉 Try to avoid… but if needed, okay
🎯 Use case:
You have a spot instance node (cheap but unstable)
You prefer not to run important pods here…but if cluster is full → fine, use it.

Step 1 — Taint the Node

kubectl taint nodes node2 spot=true:PreferNoSchedule
Meaning:
🙏 "Please avoid scheduling pods here if possible."

Step 2 — Normal Pod (No toleration)
Scheduler behaviour:
* If other nodes are free → pod goes there ✅
* If cluster is full → pod may run on node2 😅
So this is soft restriction

Step 3 — Pod with toleration

tolerations:
- key: "spot"
  value: "true"
  effect: "PreferNoSchedule"
➡️ Scheduler now happily schedules here.

🔵 3. NoExecute
👉 Don't schedule new pods AND remove existing pods
This one is dangerous 🚨

🎯 Use case:
Node becomes unhealthy or under maintenance.

Step 1 — Apply taint

kubectl taint nodes node3 maintenance=true:NoExecute
Now:
* New pods ❌ NOT scheduled
* Existing pods ❌ REMOVED immediately

Step 2 — Pod WITHOUT toleration
Running pods on node3 will be:
➡️ Evicted immediately

Step 3 — Pod WITH toleration (stay for some time)

tolerations:
- key: "maintenance"
  value: "true"
  effect: "NoExecute"
  tolerationSeconds: 60
Meaning:
"I can stay on this node for 60 seconds before eviction."
After 60 sec → pod removed.

**Node selectors:**

Its simplest way to choose a node for your pod

Example:

Suppose you have 3 nodes:

Node1->label:disk=ssd
Node2->label:disk=hsd
Node3->label:disk=bsd

Now you want your pod to run on specific node with label:ssd

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: mypod
spec:
  nodeSelector:
    disk: ssd
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx

✅ What happens?
Kubernetes checks:
“Which node has label disk=ssd?”
It finds Node 1 and Node 3.
It schedules Pod on one of them.

If pod did not get sepcific node with that label it keeps checking it will be in pending state.


**Node Affinity:**

It is more powerful version of node selector.

It allows you to use:

Use more conditions
use AND/OR logic
say "must match " or "preferred"

Two types of node affinity:

1. Required:(Hard Rule)
Pod must run on matching node

2. Preferred(Soft rule)
Pod tries to run here if not exist it can run elsewhere

# Example 1: Required (Hard Rule)

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: mypod
spec:
  affinity:
    nodeAffinity:
      requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
        nodeSelectorTerms:
        - matchExpressions:
          - key: disk
            operator: In
            values:
            - ssd
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx

Meaning:
Node must have disk=ssd
If no ssd node->pod will not run

# Example 2: Preferred (Soft Rule)

affinity:
  nodeAffinity:
    preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
    - weight: 1
      preference:
        matchExpressions:
        - key: disk
          operator: In
          values:
          - ssd
Meaning:
Try SSD node first
If not availabel-use any node


| Feature          | Taints & Tolerations | Node Affinity          |
| ---------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------- |
| Who controls?    | Node controls        | Pod controls           |
| Purpose          | Repel unwanted pods  | Attract specific nodes |
| Default behavior | Blocks pods          | Just selects nodes     |
| Main use         | Dedicated nodes      | Workload placement     |


**Resource Limits:**

1️⃣ What Are “Requests” and “Limits”?
* requests → Minimum resources your container must get
* limits → Maximum resources your container can use
Think like this:
Type	Meaning
request	Guaranteed amount
limit	Maximum allowed

🔹 2️⃣ Example: How to Define CPU & Memory
Here is a simple Deployment example:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-app
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: my-container
        image: nginx
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "200m"
            memory: "256Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "500m"
            memory: "512Mi"

🔹 3️⃣ What Do These Values Mean?
CPU Values
* 1000m = 1 CPU core
* 500m = 0.5 CPU
* 200m = 0.2 CPU
👉 m means millicpu

Memory Values
* Mi = Mebibytes
* Gi = Gibibytes
Examples:
* 256Mi ≈ 256 MB
* 1Gi = 1024 Mi

✅ Correct Order to Configure
Always set in this order:

1️⃣ Set Requests & Limits
2️⃣ Then Configure HPA
3️⃣ Probes are independent

**Daemon sets:**

Daemon set ensures that every pod run on each node in cluster.

🧠 Simple Example
Imagine you have 5 computers (nodes) in your Kubernetes cluster.
If you create a Deployment → maybe 3 pods run anywhere.
But if you create a DaemonSet →
✔ 1 pod runs on Node 1✔ 1 pod runs on Node 2✔ 1 pod runs on Node 3✔ 1 pod runs on Node 4✔ 1 pod runs on Node 5

If we remove node pod also removed in that node

📊 1️⃣ Log Collection (Very Common Use)
🧠 Problem:
Every node generates logs:
* Application logs
* System logs
* Container logs
We need to collect all logs and send them to one central place.
✅ Solution:
Run one log collector on every node.
Popular tools:
Fluentd
Filebeat
🔎 How It Works:
Each node:
* Runs one Fluentd pod
* Collects logs from that node
* Sends logs to Elasticsearch / server
👉 Without DaemonSet, some nodes may not send logs.

📡 2️⃣ Monitoring (CPU, Memory Check)
🧠 Problem:
We want to monitor:
* CPU usage
* Memory usage
* Disk usage
* Network traffic
Each node has different usage.
✅ Solution:
Install one monitoring agent on every node.
Popular tool:
* Prometheus (uses node exporters)

🔎 Example:
Each node runs:
* 1 monitoring pod
* It sends metrics to Prometheus server
If Node 3 CPU becomes 95%:👉 Monitoring agent reports it.

🌐 3️⃣ Network Plugins
🧠 Problem:
Kubernetes networking must work on every node.
Every node must:
* Handle pod IP
* Connect pods across nodes
✅ Solution:
Install network plugin on every node.
Popular examples:
* Calico
* Flannel
🔎 Why DaemonSet?
Because networking must exist on:

Node1
Node2
Node3
All nodes!
If one node doesn’t have network plugin → pods won’t communicate.

🔒 4️⃣ Security Agents
🧠 Problem:
We want to:
* Scan containers for threats
* Detect suspicious activity
* Monitor system calls
Each node must be protected.
✅ Solution:
Run one security agent per node.
Example tools:
* Falco
* Aqua Security

If malware runs on Node2:👉 Security pod on Node2 detects it.

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: nginx-daemonset
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx-container
        image: nginx
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80


**STATIC PODS:**

What are Static Pods in Kubernetes?
Static Pods are pods that are directly managed by the kubelet on a specific node, not by the Kubernetes API server or controllers.
Think of it like this 👇
* Normally:You create a Pod → Kubernetes API server manages it.
* Static Pod:You place a Pod YAML file on the node itself → the kubelet automatically creates and runs the Pod.
So the pod is tied to that specific machine (node).

Simple Example
Suppose you put this file on the node:

/etc/kubernetes/manifests/nginx.yaml

The kubelet watches this folder.
When it sees the file, it automatically creates the pod.
If you delete the file → the pod also stops.

Key Points (Very Easy)
✅ Created by kubelet directly✅ YAML file stored on the node✅ Not managed by API server✅ Runs only on that node✅ If file removed → pod deleted

Real Use in Kubernetes
Important system components run as static pods, like:
* kube-apiserver
* kube-scheduler
* kube-controller-manager
* etcd
These are usually stored in:

/etc/kubernetes/manifests/


Quick Diagram

Normal Pod
User → API Server → Scheduler → Node → Pod

Static Pod
YAML file on Node → Kubelet → Pod

Static Pods vs. DaemonSets
A common question that arises is how static pods differ from DaemonSets. The table below summarizes the key differences between the two:

| Feature | Static Pods | DaemonSets |
|--------|-------------|------------|
| Creation Source | Directly managed by the kubelet | Managed by the DaemonSet controller via the kube-apiserver |
| Control Plane Involvement | No API server interaction | Requires kube-apiserver communication |
| Use Case | Typically used for critical control plane components | Ensures a copy of a pod runs on every node |
| Interaction with Scheduler | Ignored by the kube-scheduler | Ignored by the kube-scheduler |


**PRIORITY CLASS**

In Kubernetes, Priority Classes decide which pods are more important than others when the cluster does not have enough resources (CPU/RAM).Think of it like VIP seating in a movie theatre 🎬.

1️⃣ Simple Analogy (Movie Theatre)
Imagine a theatre with limited seats:
* VIP ticket holders → always get seats first
* Regular ticket holders → get seats if seats remain
* If VIPs arrive and the theatre is full, some regular people may be asked to leave
In Kubernetes:
* VIPs = High priority pods
* Regular = Low priority pods
* Kubernetes may stop (evict) low-priority pods to run important ones.
This process is called Preemption.

2️⃣ Why Priority Classes Are Used
Priority classes help when:
* Cluster resources are limited
* Some applications are more critical
Example:
Application	Priority
Database	High
Payment service	High
Monitoring	Medium
Test jobs	Low

If resources run out, test jobs may be removed first.

3️⃣ How Priority Class Works
A PriorityClass object gives a numeric value to priority.
Example:

apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: high-priority
value: 1000
globalDefault: false
description: "High priority pods"

Meaning:
* Pods using high-priority get value 1000
* Higher number = higher priority

4️⃣ Using Priority Class in a Pod
Example pod:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: important-app
spec:
  priorityClassName: high-priority
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx

Now this pod is more important than normal pods.

5️⃣ What Happens When Cluster Is Full
Case:
1. Low-priority pod running
2. High-priority pod comes
3. No resources available
➡ Kubernetes removes low-priority pod➡ Runs high-priority pod
This is preemption.

✅ In one line:Priority Classes tell Kubernetes which pods are more important so they get resources first.



In Kubernetes, Preemption Policy decides whether a high-priority pod is allowed to remove (evict) lower-priority pods to get resources.
Think of it as permission to kick someone out of the seat.

1️⃣ Simple Example
Imagine a flight ✈️
* Business class passenger arrives
* Economy seats are full
* Airline may upgrade or move someone to make space
But sometimes the airline policy may say no passenger can be removed.
That rule = Preemption Policy.

2️⃣ Two Preemption Policy Options
1. PreemptLowerPriority (Default)
High-priority pods can remove lower-priority pods.
Example:
* Cluster full
* Low priority pod running
* High priority pod scheduled
➡ Kubernetes evicts low priority pod➡ High priority pod runs

2. Never
High-priority pods cannot evict other pods.
Example:
* Cluster full
* High priority pod arrives
* Policy = Never
➡ Pod waits in Pending state➡ No pods are removed

3️⃣ Example Pod with Preemption Policy

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: important-app
spec:
  priorityClassName: high-priority
  preemptionPolicy: Never
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx

Meaning:
* Pod has high priority
* But cannot evict other pods

4️⃣ Quick Comparison
Feature	What it does
PriorityClass	Decides importance of pods
PreemptionPolicy	Decides if lower pods can be evicted

5️⃣ Real Production Example
Example cluster:
Service	Priority	Policy
Payment service	High	PreemptLowerPriority
Monitoring	Medium	PreemptLowerPriority
Batch jobs	Low	—
ML training job	High	Never

4️⃣ Real-World Recommendation (Very Important)
Instead of random numbers, use clear gaps:
Class	Value
Low	100
Medium	1000
High	10000
Critical	100000


🚪 **Admission Controllers in Kubernetes (Complete Guide)**
🔹 What exactly are they?
👉 Admission Controllers = filters that run AFTER authentication & authorization, BEFORE object is stored
They:
* Validate requests
* Modify requests
* Enforce policies

🔹 Full Request Flow (Very Important 🔥)

kubectl apply -f pod.yaml
        ↓
Authentication (Who are you?)
        ↓
Authorization (Are you allowed?)
        ↓
👉 Admission Controllers (THIS TOPIC)
        ↓
Object stored in etcd


🔹 Two Types (Core Concept)
1️⃣ Mutating Admission Controllers
👉 They modify the request
Example
You create a Pod without limits:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: mypod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx

👉 After mutation (by LimitRanger):

containers:
- name: app
  image: nginx
  resources:
    limits:
      cpu: "500m"
      memory: "256Mi"

💡 Kubernetes automatically adds defaults

2️⃣ Validating Admission Controllers
👉 They ONLY check and approve/reject
Example

containers:
- name: app
  image: nginx
  securityContext:
    privileged: true

👉 PodSecurity says:❌ "Privileged containers not allowed"
👉 Result:

Error: forbidden


🔹 Built-in Admission Controllers (Important List)
🔸 1. NamespaceLifecycle
👉 Prevents operations in deleted namespaces
Example:
* You try to create Pod in terminating namespace → ❌ rejected

🔸 2. LimitRanger
👉 Applies default limits
Example:
* No CPU defined → auto adds CPU

🔸 3. ServiceAccount
👉 Automatically assigns service account
Example:

spec:
  serviceAccountName: default


🔸 4. ResourceQuota
👉 Enforces limits per namespace
Example quota:

apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: quota
spec:
  hard:
    pods: "2"

👉 If you create 3rd pod:❌ rejected

🔸 5. PodSecurity (VERY IMPORTANT 🔥)
👉 Replaces old PodSecurityPolicy (PSP)
Levels:
* privileged
* baseline
* restricted
Example:

metadata:
  labels:
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted

👉 Blocks:
* root containers
* privileged mode

🔥 Webhook Admission Controllers (MOST IMPORTANT FOR REAL WORLD)
🔹 What are they?
👉 You can write your own logic using HTTP services
Types:
* MutatingAdmissionWebhook
* ValidatingAdmissionWebhook

🔹 Example Use Cases
* Inject sidecars (Istio)
* Block untrusted images
* Enforce labels
* Restrict namespaces

🔹 Example: Validating Webhook
Step 1: Rule
👉 "Only allow images from company registry"

Step 2: Webhook YAML

apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingWebhookConfiguration
metadata:
  name: validate-images
webhooks:
- name: validate.k8s.io
  rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    apiVersions: ["v1"]
    operations: ["CREATE"]
    resources: ["pods"]
  clientConfig:
    service:
      name: webhook-service
      namespace: default
      path: "/validate"
  admissionReviewVersions: ["v1"]
  sideEffects: None


Step 3: Logic (Pseudo-code)

if not image.startswith("mycompany/"):
    reject()

👉 Result:
* nginx → ❌ rejected
* mycompany/nginx → ✅ allowed

🔹 Example: Mutating Webhook
👉 Auto-add sidecar container
Before:

containers:
- name: app
  image: nginx

After mutation:

containers:
- name: app
  image: nginx
- name: sidecar
  image: logging-agent


🔹 Order of Execution (Important)
1. Mutating controllers run first
2. Then validating controllers
👉 Flow:

Request
 → Mutate (modify)
 → Validate (check)
 → Store


🔹 How to Enable Admission Controllers
In kube-apiserver:

--enable-admission-plugins=LimitRanger,ResourceQuota,PodSecurity


🔹 Real-world Example Scenario
👉 Company rule:
* All pods must have:
    * labels
    * resource limits
    * approved images
👉 Solution:
* LimitRanger → adds defaults
* ResourceQuota → restrict usage
* PodSecurity → enforce security
* Webhook → enforce company rules

