📘 **Rolling Updates & Rollbacks (OpenShift / OC) — Easy Notes**


🔹 1. Rollout & Revision (Same in OpenShift)
* When you create deployment → rollout starts
* First version → Revision 1
* After update → Revision 2
👉 OpenShift tracks versions automatically

✅ Check rollout status

oc rollout status deployment/myapp

✅ See rollout history

oc rollout history deployment/myapp


🔹 2. Deployment Strategies
🟥 Recreate
* Deletes all old pods
* Then creates new pods
❌ App DOWN (downtime)

🟩 Rolling Update (DEFAULT)
* Updates pods one by one
✅ No downtime✅ Safe

🔹 3. Create Deployment (OC Example)

oc create deployment myapp --image=nginx:1.7.0

👉 Check:

oc get pods


🔹 4. Update Deployment (IMPORTANT)
✅ Method 1: Update image

oc set image deployment/myapp nginx=nginx:1.9.1

👉 This triggers:
* New rollout
* New revision

✅ Check rollout

oc rollout status deployment/myapp


🔹 5. View Details

oc describe deployment myapp

👉 Shows:
* Strategy
* ReplicaSets
* Events

🔹 6. Real Example (STEP-BY-STEP 🔥)
Step 1: Create deployment

oc create deployment myapp --image=nginx:1.7.0


Step 2: Expose service (optional)

oc expose deployment myapp --port=80


Step 3: Update image (simulate upgrade)

oc set image deployment/myapp nginx=nginx:1.9.1

👉 What happens:
* New pods start
* Old pods slowly stop
* No downtime

Step 4: Watch rollout

oc rollout status deployment/myapp


Step 5: Check history

oc rollout history deployment/myapp

👉 Output shows:
* Revision 1 → nginx:1.7.0
* Revision 2 → nginx:1.9.1

🔹 7. Rollback (VERY IMPORTANT)
👉 If update fails

oc rollout undo deployment/myapp

👉 What happens:
* New pods deleted
* Old pods restored

Check again:

oc get pods


🔹 8. Check ReplicaSets

oc get rs

👉 You will see:
* Old version RS
* New version RS

🔹 9. Quick Command Revision
Task	OC Command
Create deployment	oc create deployment myapp --image=nginx
Get deployments	oc get deployments
Update image	oc set image deployment/myapp nginx=nginx:1.9.1
Check rollout	oc rollout status deployment/myapp
Rollback	oc rollout undo deployment/myapp
History	oc rollout history deployment/myapp
🔹 10. SUPER SIMPLE FLOW 🧠
1. Create deployment
2. Update image → rollout starts
3. New version created
4. If issue → rollback

🔥 Final Real-Life Example (CRC)

# Create
oc create deployment webapp --image=nginx:1.7.0

# Update
oc set image deployment/webapp nginx=nginx:1.9.1

# Check rollout
oc rollout status deployment/webapp

# See history
oc rollout history deployment/webapp

# Rollback if error
oc rollout undo deployment/webapp


⭐ Exam / Interview Tips
* Default strategy → Rolling Update
* Rollout = deployment update
* Revision = version
* Rollback = go back to old version
* OpenShift uses same Kubernetes logic


🧠 **OC/CRC: Kubernetes Commands & Arguments**
🔹 Core Idea
Kubernetes lets you change container behavior by overriding Dockerfile defaults using:
* command → overrides ENTRYPOINT
* args → overrides CMD

🔹 Docker vs Kubernetes Mapping
Docker Concept	Kubernetes Field	Purpose
ENTRYPOINT	command	Main command to run
CMD	args	Default parameters
🔹 Default Behavior Example
Dockerfile:

ENTRYPOINT ["sleep"]
CMD ["5"]

👉 Container runs:

sleep 5


🔹 Override CMD (change argument)
Docker:

docker run ubuntu-sleeper 10

Kubernetes:

args: ["10"]

👉 Result:

sleep 10


🔹 Override ENTRYPOINT (change command)
Docker:

--entrypoint sleep2.0

Kubernetes:

command: ["sleep2.0"]
args: ["10"]

👉 Result:

sleep2.0 10


🔹 Key Rules (VERY IMPORTANT)
* args → only changes parameters (CMD)
* command → completely replaces ENTRYPOINT
* Both can be used together

🔹 Deployment Command

kubectl create -f pod-definition.yml


🔹 Quick Memory Trick 🧩
* command = what to run
* args = how to run

**ENV Variable:**

env:
  name: APP-color
  value: pink

  name: APP-color
  valueFrom: 
    configMapkeyRef:

  name: APP-color
  valueFrom: 
    secretkeyRef:

**📦 ConfigMap in OpenShift (CRC)**
Same concept as Kubernetes:
👉 ConfigMap = store configuration (key-value data)👉 Used by Pods/Deployments

⚡ Imperative Commands (oc)
1. Create ConfigMap (literal)

oc create configmap my-config \
  --from-literal=APP_COLOR=blue \
  --from-literal=APP_MODE=prod


2. Create from file

oc create configmap my-config \
  --from-file=config.properties


3. Create from env file

oc create configmap my-config \
  --from-env-file=app.env

Example:

APP_COLOR=blue
APP_MODE=prod


4. View ConfigMap

oc get configmap
oc describe configmap my-config


5. Edit ConfigMap

oc edit configmap my-config


6. Delete ConfigMap

oc delete configmap my-config


📄 Declarative (YAML - oc apply)
1. ConfigMap YAML

apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: my-config
data:
  APP_COLOR: blue
  APP_MODE: prod


2. Apply YAML

oc apply -f configmap.yaml


3. Update ConfigMap

oc apply -f configmap.yaml


4. Delete using YAML

oc delete -f configmap.yaml


🔗 Use ConfigMap in Pod (OpenShift)
As Environment Variable

env:
  - name: APP_COLOR
    valueFrom:
      configMapKeyRef:
        name: my-config
        key: APP_COLOR


As Volume

volumes:
  - name: config-volume
    configMap:
      name: my-config


🧠 Important Points (CRC / OpenShift)
* oc = same as kubectl but for OpenShift
* Works inside a project (namespace)👉 check project:

envFrom:
  - configMapRef:
      name: my-config


**🔐 How to Encrypt Secret Data (Easy Idea)**

🎯 Goal (What you are doing)
👉 You will:
1. Create a secret (not secure ❌)
2. Enable encryption 🔐
3. Create new secret (secure ✅)

🧪 FULL LAB – OpenShift CRC (Step-by-Step)

🔹 STEP 1: Start CRC and Login

oc get apiserver cluster -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}'

crc start
oc login -u kubeadmin -p <your-password>

Check:

oc get nodes

👉 If node shows → OK ✅

🔹 STEP 2: Create Secret (NOT Encrypted)

oc create secret generic my-secret \
  --from-literal=password=mypassword


🔹 STEP 3: See Secret (Understand Problem)

oc get secret my-secret -o yaml

You will see:

password: bXlwYXNzd29yZA==

👉 This is NOT encryption👉 Just encoding (easy to decode) 

🔹 STEP 4: Enable Encryption (MAIN STEP)
Run:

oc edit apiserver cluster

👉 An editor opens

👉 Inside that file:
Find spec:If not present, create it.
Add this:

spec:
  encryption:
    type: aescbc


👉 Example full look:

apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1
kind: APIServer
metadata:
  name: cluster
spec:
  encryption:
    type: aescbc


👉 Save and exit:
* Press ESC
* Type :wq
* Enter

🔹 STEP 5: Wait (Very Important ⏳)
Run:

oc get apiserver cluster -o yaml

Wait until you see:

type: EncryptionCompleted
status: "True"

👉 This means encryption is ON ✅

🔹 STEP 6: Create NEW Secret (Encrypted)

oc create secret generic my-secret-2 \
  --from-literal=password=topsecret


🔹 STEP 7: Verify (Simple Way)
Check again:

oc get secret my-secret-2 -o yaml

👉 Still looks base64 (normal)
BUT internally:👉 Stored encrypted in etcd ✅

🔹 STEP 8: Re-encrypt OLD Secrets
Old secret is still NOT encrypted ❗
Run:

oc get secrets --all-namespaces -o json | oc replace -f -

👉 Now all secrets become encrypted ✅

🧠 SUPER SIMPLE UNDERSTANDING
Think like this:
Before:
* Secret = "password"
* Stored = base64 → easy to decode ❌
After:
* Secret = "password"
* Stored = encrypted → cannot read 🔐

🔥 Most Important Commands (Remember)

oc edit apiserver cluster

Add:

spec:
  encryption:
    type: aescbc


💡 Easy Understanding
Think like this:
* You want to lock your data 🔐
* But you must choose which lock type
👉 aescbc = strong lock 🔒

🔍 What is AES?
👉 AES = Advanced Encryption Standard
* Very secure 🔐
* Used in:
    * Banking 💳
    * HTTPS 🌐
    * VPN 🔑

🔍 What is CBC?
👉 CBC = Cipher Block Chaining
👉 Simple idea:
* Data is split into blocks
* Each block depends on previous block
👉 So:
* Hard to break ❌
* More secure ✅

📦 Why we use aescbc in this lab?
Because:
✔️ Supported by Kubernetes & OpenShift✔️ Easy to configure✔️ Strong security✔️ Default recommended method


🧩 **Multi-Container Pod Design Patterns (Complete Notes)**
In Kubernetes, a multi-container pod means:
👉 Running multiple containers inside a single Pod that work together as one unit.
📌 Key Properties
Same IP & network (localhost communication)
Shared volumes
Start/stop together (same lifecycle)
Scaling happens at Pod level
🔧 1. Sidecar Pattern
🧠 Concept
A sidecar container runs alongside the main container and adds supporting functionality.
👉 Main app = business logic
👉 Sidecar = helper (logging, monitoring, proxy, etc.)
📦 Example (App + Logging Sidecar)
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: sidecar-example
spec:
  volumes:
    - name: log-volume
      emptyDir: {}

  containers:
    - name: app
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c"]
      args:
        - while true; do
            echo "App running" >> /var/log/app.log;
            sleep 5;
          done
      volumeMounts:
        - name: log-volume
          mountPath: /var/log

    - name: log-sidecar
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c"]
      args:
        - tail -f /var/log/app.log
      volumeMounts:
        - name: log-volume
          mountPath: /var/log
✅ When to Use
Logging (Fluentd)
Monitoring (Prometheus exporters)
Service mesh proxies (Istio Envoy)
🌐 2. Ambassador Pattern
🧠 Concept
An ambassador container acts as a proxy between your app and external services.
👉 App talks to localhost
👉 Ambassador forwards to external system
📦 Example (DB Proxy)
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: ambassador-example
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c"]
      args:
        - nc localhost 5432

    - name: ambassador
      image: alpine/socat
      command: ["sh", "-c"]
      args:
        - socat TCP-LISTEN:5432,fork TCP:mydb.example.com:5432
✅ When to Use
External DB/API access
Simplify configuration
Handle retries, TLS, routing
🔄 3. Adapter Pattern
🧠 Concept
An adapter container transforms data into a format required by external systems.
👉 Converts output → standard format
📦 Example (Log Transformation)
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: adapter-example
spec:
  volumes:
    - name: shared-data
      emptyDir: {}

  containers:
    - name: app
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c"]
      args:
        - while true; do
            echo "user login" >> /data/raw.log;
            sleep 5;
          done
      volumeMounts:
        - name: shared-data
          mountPath: /data

    - name: adapter
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c"]
      args:
        - tail -f /data/raw.log | sed 's/^/{ "log": "&" }/'
      volumeMounts:
        - name: shared-data
          mountPath: /data
✅ When to Use
Convert logs → JSON
Convert metrics → Prometheus format
Standardize output
🚀 4. Init Container Pattern
🧠 Concept
Init containers run before main containers start.
👉 Used for setup tasks
📦 Example (Preload Config)
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: init-container-example
spec:
  volumes:
    - name: config-volume
      emptyDir: {}

  initContainers:
    - name: init-setup
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c"]
      args:
        - echo "config ready" > /config/config.txt
      volumeMounts:
        - name: config-volume
          mountPath: /config

  containers:
    - name: app
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c"]
      args:
        - cat /config/config.txt && sleep 3600
      volumeMounts:
        - name: config-volume
          mountPath: /config
✅ When to Use


**🧠 1. What are CPU & Resource Limits (Simple)**
In Kubernetes:
* Requests = minimum resources your app needs
* Limits = maximum resources your app can use
Example:

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "200m"
    memory: "256Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"

👉 Meaning:
* App guaranteed 0.2 CPU & 256MB RAM
* App cannot exceed 0.5 CPU & 512MB RAM

**🧭 2. How to Decide Values (Step-by-Step EASY WAY)**
✅ Step 1: Start WITHOUT limits (testing phase)
Deploy your app first with no limits or very high limits
👉 Why?To understand how much it actually uses

✅ Step 2: Monitor usage
Use:
* kubectl top pod
* or monitoring tools like Prometheus + Grafana
Look at:
* CPU usage
* Memory usage

✅ Step 3: Take average + peak
Example:
* Avg CPU = 150m
* Peak CPU = 300m
* Avg Memory = 200Mi
* Peak Memory = 350Mi

✅ Step 4: Define requests (safe baseline)
👉 Rule:
* Requests ≈ average usage
So:

requests:
  cpu: "150m"
  memory: "200Mi"


✅ Step 5: Define limits (buffer)
👉 Rule:
* Limits ≈ peak × 1.5 (buffer)
So:

limits:
  cpu: "450m"
  memory: "500Mi"


📊 3. Simple Formula (Remember This)
👉 Requests = Average usage👉 Limits = Peak usage × 1.5 (or 2x)

HPA(Horizontal Pod Aotoscaler)

🚀 **Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) — Complete Guide**

🧠 What is HPA?
👉 HPA automatically increases or decreases the number of pods based on load (CPU / Memory).

⚙️ How HPA Works (Simple Flow)
1. You set a target (CPU or Memory %)
2. Kubernetes checks current usage
3. HPA decides:
    * Increase pods 📈 (scale up)
    * Decrease pods 📉 (scale down)

📦 Basic Requirements
Before using HPA:
✔ Deployment must exist✔ Resource requests must be set✔ Metrics Server must be installed
Check:

kubectl top pods


⚙️ Example 1: HPA with CPU only

apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: cpu-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: my-app

  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10

  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 50


📊 How it behaves
* CPU > 50% → scale up
* CPU < 50% → scale down

⚙️ Example 2: HPA with Memory only

apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: memory-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: my-app

  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10

  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: memory
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 60


📊 How it behaves
* Memory > 60% → scale up
* Memory < 60% → scale down (slowly)

⚙️ Example 3: HPA with CPU + Memory (Most Important)

apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: cpu-memory-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: my-app

  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10

  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 50

  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: memory
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 60


🧠 Important Rule
👉 HPA checks both CPU and Memory
👉 It scales based on:➡️ whichever needs more pods

📊 Real-Time Scenarios
🟢 Case 1: Normal load
* CPU = 40%
* Memory = 50%👉 No scaling

🔥 Case 2: High CPU
* CPU = 80%
* Memory = 50%
👉 Scale up (CPU triggered)

🔥 Case 3: High Memory
* CPU = 45%
* Memory = 85%
👉 Scale up (Memory triggered)

🌙 Case 4: Low usage
* CPU = 20%
* Memory = 30%
👉 Scale down

⚠️ Common Mistakes
❌ Not setting resources.requests❌ Metrics server not installed❌ Expecting memory to drop quickly❌ Setting wrong thresholds (too low/high)

🔍 Useful Commands

kubectl get hpa
kubectl describe hpa cpu-memory-hpa
kubectl top pods


🧠 CPU vs Memory (Quick Recap)
* CPU → fast changing → best for traffic
* Memory → slow changing → used for stability

💡 When to use what?
✔ Use CPU:
* Web apps
* APIs
* Traffic-based systems
✔ Use Memory:
* Caching apps
* Data-heavy apps
* Java apps
✔ Use both:
* Production systems (recommended)


🚀 HPA vs VPA (Easy Differences)
Point	HPA 🟢	VPA 🔵
Full form	Horizontal Pod Autoscaler	Vertical Pod Autoscaler
What it changes	Number of pods	CPU & Memory of pod
Scaling type	Horizontal	Vertical
Example	2 → 6 pods	200Mi → 600Mi memory
Restart needed	❌ No	⚠️ Yes (sometimes)
Speed	Fast	Slow
Best for	Traffic increase	Resource optimization
Use case	Web apps, APIs	Databases, heavy apps
🧠 Super Simple Line
👉 HPA = increase pods👉 VPA = increase pod size

📦 VPA Notes (Very Easy)
🧠 What VPA does
👉 Automatically adjusts:
* CPU
* Memory
based on real usage

⚙️ VPA Modes
1. Off mode
👉 Only gives recommendation👉 No changes

2. Initial mode
👉 Sets values when pod starts👉 No changes later

3. Auto mode (important)
👉 Automatically updates resources👉 May restart pod

⚠️ Important Points
* VPA may restart pods to apply changes
* Works best for stable workloads
* Not good for sudden traffic spikes
* Needs metrics server

📊 Simple Example
Before VPA:

cpu: 200m
memory: 200Mi

After VPA:

cpu: 500m
memory: 600Mi


💡 When to use VPA
✔ You don’t know correct CPU/memory✔ App runs for long time✔ Want to save cost (no over-allocation)

⚠️ One Important Interview Point
👉 Don’t use HPA + VPA together blindly👉 They can conflict (both try to control CPU)

🧠 Final Quick Revision
* HPA → handles traffic
* VPA → handles resource sizing

**VPA is not built in for kubernetes**

**OS Upgradation**

Kubernetes (K8s) OS upgradation usually means updating the operating system on the nodes (machines) in your cluster without breaking your running applications.
Let’s break it down in very simple terms 👇
🧠 Basic Idea
A Kubernetes cluster has:
Master (Control Plane) nodes
Worker nodes (where your apps run)
OS upgrade = updating Linux (like Ubuntu, CentOS, etc.) on these nodes.
But ⚠️ you must not stop everything at once, or your apps will go down.
So Kubernetes uses a rolling upgrade approach.

🔄 **How OS Upgradation Works (Step-by-Step)**
1. Pick one node at a time
You never upgrade all nodes together.
👉 Upgrade happens node by node
2. Drain the node
Before upgrading, Kubernetes safely moves apps away:
kubectl drain <node-name>
What happens:
Pods are gracefully stopped
They are moved to other nodes
3. Upgrade the OS
Now you can safely upgrade OS on that node:
Example (Ubuntu):
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo reboot
4. Bring node back
After reboot:
kubectl uncordon <node-name>
Now:
Node becomes active again
New pods can run on it
5. Repeat for next node
Same process for all nodes one by one
🔁 Why This Works (Important Concept)
Kubernetes ensures:
Apps are running in multiple replicas
So when one node goes down → others handle traffic
This is called high availability
⚠️ Important Things to Remember
✅ 1. Always have multiple replicas
Use Deployments like:
replicas: 3
✅ 2. Use Pod Disruption Budget (PDB)
It ensures minimum pods stay running
✅ 3. Control Plane first or last?
In production: usually workers first, then control plane carefully
✅ 4. Managed Kubernetes (EKS, GKE, AKS)
Cloud providers often:
Handle node upgrades automatically
Or provide rolling upgrade tools
🧩 Simple Analogy
Think of a hotel 🏨:
Rooms = Nodes
Guests = Pods
To renovate a room:
Move guests to other rooms
Renovate
Open it again
Do this one room at a time → hotel keeps running
🚀 Quick Summary
Upgrade nodes one by one
Use:
kubectl drain
upgrade OS
kubectl uncordon
Kubernetes automatically reschedules apps
Ensures zero downtime (if configured well)


🚀 Kubernetes Cluster Upgrade — Real-Time Flow (Simple)
Think of your Kubernetes cluster like this:
* 🧠 Master node = brain (control center)
* 👷 Worker nodes = workers (run your apps)

🟢 STEP 1: Before Upgrade (Current Situation)
👉 Example:
* Cluster is running version 1.10
* New versions available: 1.11, 1.12
⚠️ Important rule:
* Don’t jump directly → always go one version at a time
✅ So flow will be:

1.10 → 1.11 → 1.12


🟡 STEP 2: Start Upgrade (Master First)
👉 First upgrade the master node (brain)
What happens in real-time:
1. You run:kubeadm upgrade plan👉 It shows:
    * current version
    * next version
    * what will be upgraded
2. Then you upgrade:kubeadm upgrade apply v1.11

⚡ During this time:
* ❌ You cannot manage cluster(kubectl commands may not work)
* ✅ BUT apps are still running on worker nodes
👉 Think like:
Brain is restarting, but workers keep working

🟢 STEP 3: After Master Upgrade
Now:
* Master = ✅ upgraded (1.11)
* Workers = ❌ still old (1.10)
👉 Cluster still works fine

🔵 STEP 4: Upgrade Master’s Kubelet
👉 Important detail:
Even after upgrade:

kubectl get nodes

👉 May still show old version 😅
Why?
* Because it shows kubelet version, not API server
👉 So you:
1. Upgrade kubelet
2. Restart kubelet
Now master is fully updated ✅

🟣 STEP 5: Upgrade Worker Nodes (One by One)
👉 This is VERY important for zero downtime

🔁 For each worker node:
Step A: Drain node

kubectl drain node-1

👉 What happens:
* Moves apps (pods) to other nodes
* Node becomes empty

Step B: Upgrade node
* Upgrade kubeadm
* Upgrade kubelet
* Restart kubelet

Step C: Bring node back

kubectl uncordon node-1

👉 Node starts accepting workloads again

🔁 Repeat for all worker nodes

🔴 Alternative (Other Strategies)
Instead of one-by-one, you can:
1. Upgrade all at once
* ❌ Fast
* ❌ Causes downtime
2. Add new nodes (best in production)
* Add new nodes with new version
* Move apps
* Delete old nodes

🧠 Important Rules (Easy Memory)
✅ Rule 1:
No component > API Server version

✅ Rule 2:
Upgrade order:

1. Master
2. Worker nodes


✅ Rule 3:
Always upgrade:

One version at a time


✅ Rule 4:
kubeadm ≠ kubelet👉 You must upgrade kubelet separately

🎯 Final Real-Time Flow (Quick Summary)

START
↓
Check upgrade plan
↓
Upgrade kubeadm
↓
Upgrade MASTER (API server, scheduler, etc.)
↓
Upgrade master kubelet
↓
For each worker:
    Drain node
    Upgrade kubelet
    Uncordon node
↓
Cluster fully upgraded 🎉





**Kubernetes Backup & Restore — OC-CRC Notes**

🔹 OC1: What to Backup
C (Command / Concept):
* Backup:
    * Declarative files (YAML)
    * Cluster state (etcd)
    * Imperative resources (created manually)
R (Reason):
* If cluster fails → you can recreate everything
C (Conclusion):👉 Always keep YAML + etcd backup for safety 

🔹 OC2: Declarative Backup (BEST METHOD)
C:

kubectl apply -f pod-definition.yml

R:
* YAML files act like documentation + backup
* Can store in GitHub
C:👉 Easy restore → just re-apply files

🔹 OC3: Imperative Backup (From Cluster)
C:

kubectl get all --all-namespaces -o yaml > backup.yaml

R:
* Some resources created using commands (not saved in files)
C:👉 This command exports full cluster state

🔹 OC4: etcd Backup (MOST IMPORTANT)
C:

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save snapshot.db

R:
* etcd stores all cluster data (brain of Kubernetes)
C:👉 Without etcd → cluster cannot recover

🔹 OC5: Check Backup
C:

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status snapshot.db

R:
* Verify snapshot is valid
C:👉 Always verify backup after taking it

🔹 OC6: etcd Restore Steps (IMPORTANT LAB)
C:
1. Stop API server
2. Restore snapshot:

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore snapshot.db \
--data-dir /var/lib/etcd-from-backup

1. Update etcd config (new data-dir)
2. Restart services
R:
* Restore creates new etcd data directory
C:👉 Cluster comes back from backup

🔹 OC7: Secure Backup (with certs)
C:

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save snapshot.db \
--endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
--cacert=/etc/etcd/ca.crt \
--cert=/etc/etcd/server.crt \
--key=/etc/etcd/server.key

R:
* etcd uses TLS → needs certificates
C:👉 Required in real clusters

🔹 OC8: Which Method to Use?
Method	When to Use
YAML (Declarative)	Best & easiest
kubectl get backup	When resources not saved
etcd snapshot	Full cluster recovery
👉 Final idea:
* YAML = App backup
* etcd = Full cluster backup

🔥 Lab Quick Practice (IMPORTANT)
Try these:

# 1. Export cluster
kubectl get all --all-namespaces -o yaml > backup.yaml

# 2. Take etcd snapshot
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save snapshot.db

# 3. Check snapshot
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status snapshot.db


🔐 **Kubernetes Authentication — Full Easy Summary**
🧠 What this page is about
This page explains how Kubernetes checks “who you are” before allowing access to the cluster.
👉 In simple words:Before anyone (human or machine) can use Kubernetes → it must verify identity (authentication).

🏗️ Basic Idea: How Kubernetes Works
￼
￼
￼
4
* Kubernetes has a central component called kube-apiserver
* Every request goes through it:
    * kubectl
    * dashboard
    * scripts
    * bots
* First step → Authentication (Who are you?)
* Next step → Authorization (What can you do?)

👥 Who uses Kubernetes?
There are 4 types of users:
1. 👨‍💻 Admins – manage cluster
2. 🧑‍💻 Developers – deploy apps
3. 👤 End users – use apps (outside Kubernetes scope)
4. 🤖 Bots/Services – automated systems

⚠️ Important Concept
👉 Kubernetes does NOT create normal user accounts
* No kubectl create user
* Users come from outside systems
But it DOES manage:
* ✅ Service Accounts (for apps/bots inside cluster)
Example:

kubectl create serviceaccount sa1


🔑 How Authentication Works (Main Part)
Flow (very important)
1. User sends request → API server
2. API server checks identity using configured method
3. If valid → moves to authorization (RBAC)

🧩 Authentication Methods
￼
￼
￼
4
1. ❌ Static Username & Password (Basic Auth)
* Stored in CSV file
* Plain text passwords 😱
* Example:password,username,uid,groups
* ❌ Deprecated (not safe)

2. ❌ Static Token File
* Token mapped to user
* Also stored in CSV
* Used like:Authorization: Bearer <token>
* ❌ Also deprecated

3. ✅ TLS Client Certificates (Recommended)
* Uses certificates signed by CA
* Very secure
* Used for:
    * admins
    * machines
👉 Best for production systems

4. ✅ External Identity Providers (Best for big setups)
Examples:
* OIDC (Google login style)
* LDAP
* Kerberos
👉 Used in companies for:
* Single Sign-On (SSO)
* centralized login system

📊 Quick Comparison
Method	Security	Use Case
Basic Auth	❌ Low	Testing only
Token File	❌ Low	Temporary setups
TLS Cert	✅ High	Production
OIDC / LDAP	✅ Very High	Enterprise
⚙️ How It Works Internally
Example: Request Flow

kubectl command
     ↓
kube-apiserver
     ↓
Authenticate user
     ↓
Authorize (RBAC)
     ↓
Allow / Deny


Example Commands
Basic Auth (old way)

curl -u user:password https://api-server

Token


TLS Basics – Simple Summary
What is TLS?
TLS (Transport Layer Security) is used to secure communication over networks.
It ensures:
Data is encrypted
The server is authentic (trusted)
Why TLS is Needed
Without TLS, data (like passwords) is sent as plain text
Hackers can easily intercept and misuse sensitive data
Types of Encryption
1. Symmetric Encryption
Uses one single key for:
Encryption
Decryption
Problem:
Key must be shared → can be intercepted
2. Asymmetric Encryption
Uses two keys:
Public key (shared)
Private key (kept secret)
Data encrypted with public key → decrypted only by private key
More secure for key exchange
SSH Security Using Key Pairs
Instead of passwords, SSH uses key pairs
Steps:
Generate keys using ssh-keygen
Private key: id_rsa
Public key: id_rsa.pub
Add public key to server (authorized_keys)
Connect using private key:
ssh -i id_rsa user@server
Same private key can be used across multiple servers
How HTTPS (TLS) Works
Server creates public + private key
Server sends certificate (with public key) to client
Client generates a symmetric key
Client encrypts it using server’s public key
Server decrypts using its private key
Communication continues using symmetric encryption
👉 Combines:
Asymmetric → secure key exchange
Symmetric → fast communication
Generating Keys (OpenSSL)
# Private key
openssl genrsa -out my-bank.key 1024

# Public key
openssl rsa -in my-bank.key -pubout > mybank.pem
Certificates
A certificate contains:
Issuer (who signed it)
Domain name
Public key
Validity period
Certificate Authorities (CAs)
Trusted organizations (e.g., DigiCert, GlobalSign)
They:
Verify identity
Sign certificates
Certificate Signing Request (CSR)
openssl req -new -key my-bank.key -out my-bank.csr \
-subj "/C=US/ST=CA/O=MyOrg/CN=my-bank.com"
Sent to CA to get a signed certificate
Browser Trust Process
Server sends certificate
Browser checks it using CA public keys
If valid → secure connection established
Internal Systems
Companies can create their own private CA
Used for internal applications
Important Notes
Public key → can be shared
Private key → must be kept secret
Files:
Certificates → .crt, .pem
Private keys → .key
Key Takeaways
TLS secures communication using encryption
Uses both:
Asymmetric (for key exchange)
Symmetric (for data transfer)
SSH uses key pairs instead of passwords
HTTPS relies on CA-signed certificates

🔐 **TLS in Kubernetes – Notes**
📌 Overview
TLS (Transport Layer Security) is used to secure communication in a Kubernetes cluster.
It ensures:
Encryption 🔒
Authentication ✅
Uses:
Server Certificates → Secure services
Client Certificates → Authenticate users/components
📁 Certificate File Naming
Public Certificates
Extensions: .crt, .pem
Example: server.crt, client.pem
Private Keys
Contain word key
Example: server.key, server-key.pem
👉 If filename doesn’t contain key, it’s usually a public certificate
🧩 Kubernetes Components & Certificates
🖥️ Server Components (Use Server Certificates)
Kube API Server
Main control plane component
Example:
api-server.crt
api-server.key
ETCD Server
Stores cluster data
Example:
etcd-server.crt
etcd-server.key
Kubelet (Worker Nodes)
Runs on each worker node
Example:
kubelet.crt
kubelet.key
👤 Client Components (Use Client Certificates)
Admin (kubectl)
Example:
admin.crt
admin.key
Scheduler
Example:
scheduler.crt
scheduler.key
Controller Manager
Uses its own certificate pair
Kube Proxy
Example:
kube-proxy.crt
kube-proxy.key
🔄 Communication Flow
API Server communicates with:
ETCD (secure connection)
Kubelets (secure connection)
Can use:
Same certificates OR
Separate dedicated certificates
📂 Certificate Categories
Type	Used By
Client Certificates	Admin, Scheduler, Controller Manager, Kube Proxy
Server Certificates	API Server, ETCD, Kubelet
🏢 Certificate Authority (CA)
All certificates must be signed by a CA
CA has:
CA.crt
CA.key
You can use:
Single CA (simple setup)
Multiple CAs (advanced security)
🧠 Key Points to Remember
TLS = Security backbone of Kubernetes
Every component needs:
Certificate + Key
Two main types:
Client certs (authentication)
Server certs (encryption)
CA is mandatory



🔐 **What is this about?**
This guide explains how to create TLS certificates in Kubernetes using OpenSSL.
👉 TLS certificates help secure communication between different parts of a Kubernetes cluster.
🧩 1. Certificate Authority (CA)
First, you create a CA (Certificate Authority).
CA is like a trusted authority that signs all other certificates.
It generates:
ca.key → private key (keep it safe!)
ca.crt → root certificate
👉 All other certificates will be signed using this CA.
👤 2. Client Certificates (Users & Components)
Used by:
Admin user
Scheduler
Controller Manager
kube-proxy
Example: Admin certificate
Create private key
Create CSR (request)
Sign it using CA
👉 This certificate acts like login credentials (username + password).
🌐 3. Using Certificates
Instead of username/password, Kubernetes uses certificates.
Example: API requests use:
client key
client certificate
CA certificate
👉 These are usually stored in a kubeconfig file.
🖥️ 4. Server Certificates
Servers also need certificates.
Both client and server must trust the same CA.
📦 Etcd Server
Etcd (database of Kubernetes) needs certificates.
If multiple etcd nodes → need peer certificates.
Config file includes:
cert file
key file
trusted CA
👉 Ensures secure communication between etcd nodes.
🚪 5. Kube API Server Certificates
API server is the main entry point.
It has many names (DNS + IP).
👉 So its certificate must include:
Multiple DNS names
Multiple IP addresses (SANs)
If not → communication may fail.
🖧 6. Kubelet (Node) Certificates
Each node (server) has a kubelet.
Every node needs:
its own certificate
its own key
👉 Used for:
talking to API server
identifying the node
Also:
Node identity should be like system:node
Helps Kubernetes assign permissions correctly.


🧾 Summary: Viewing & Verifying Kubernetes Certificates
🔹 1. Purpose of the Lesson
Learn how to check and verify certificates in a Kubernetes cluster
Useful when doing health checks or troubleshooting issues
🔹 2. Understand Cluster Setup
Kubernetes clusters can be created in two ways:
Manual setup
You create and manage certificates yourself
Using kubeadm
Certificates are automatically created and managed
Components run as pods (not system services)
🔹 3. Where to Find Certificate Info
Check configuration files to locate certificates:
Manual setup → system service files
kubeadm → /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
Example:
kube-apiserver config shows paths like:
--client-ca-file
--tls-cert-file
--tls-private-key-file
🔹 4. Create a Certificate Checklist 📋
Make a list (spreadsheet recommended) with:
Certificate file path
Names (CN & SAN)
Organization
Owner
Issuer (CA)
Expiry date
👉 Helps in troubleshooting and renewal
🔹 5. Inspect Certificates (Important Step)
Use OpenSSL command:
openssl x509 -in <certificate-file> -text -noout
This shows:
Certificate details
Expiry date
Issuer
Valid names
✔ Ensure:
Not expired
Correct CA
Proper configuration
🔹 6. Troubleshooting Using Logs 🔍
If using system services:
journalctl -u etcd.service -l
If using kubeadm:
Use:
kubectl logs <pod-name>
If cluster is down:
docker ps -a
docker logs <container-id>
⚠️ Common error:
TLS / certificate issues (e.g., “bad certificate”)
🔹 7. Key Takeaways
Always identify certificate files first
Use OpenSSL to inspect them
Check expiry + issuer
Use logs to debug issues
Maintain a certificate inventory


📌 What this page is about
It explains how certificates are managed in Kubernetes using something called the Certificates API.
🔐 Basic idea (in simple words)
Kubernetes uses certificates for security (like login IDs).
Each user/admin needs their own certificate + key to access the cluster.
These certificates are created and signed by a Certificate Authority (CA).
🔄 How the process works (step-by-step)
1. New user joins
Creates:
Private key
CSR (Certificate Signing Request)
2. Admin checks request
Admin reviews the CSR
Signs it using CA
3. User gets access
User receives signed certificate
Now they can access the cluster
4. Renewal
Certificates expire → need to repeat process
🤖 Problem with manual process
If many users → too many requests
Manual signing becomes slow and difficult
⚙️ Solution: Certificates API
Kubernetes provides Certificates API to automate things:
Users send CSR using API
Admin can:
View requests → kubectl get csr
Approve → kubectl certificate approve <name>
Kubernetes automatically signs certificates
🧠 Who actually signs certificates?
👉 Controller Manager
Handles:
CSR approval
Certificate signing
Uses CA key + certificate internally
🔑 Important points
CA = very important → must be secure
Certificates:
Give access
Expire after time
Certificates API:
Makes process automatic and scalable
✅ Final simple takeaway
👉 Kubernetes uses certificates for secure access
👉 Users request certificates via CSR
👉 Admin approves them
👉 Certificates API + Controller Manager automate everything
If you want, I can make a diagram or flowchart so it's even easier to remember 👍
give me example as well
Got it 👍 — here’s a simple real-life example so you can understand clearly.
👩‍💻 Example: New developer joining a team
Imagine you have a Kubernetes cluster and a new developer named Jane joins.
🔐 Step-by-step example
🟢 Step 1: Jane creates request
Jane creates:
Private key → jane.key
CSR (request)
👉 This is like:
“Hey admin, please give me access”
🟡 Step 2: Jane sends request
Jane sends her CSR to Kubernetes (via API)
🔵 Step 3: Admin checks request
Admin runs:
kubectl get csr
Output:
NAME   STATUS    USER
jane   Pending   jane
👉 Meaning: Jane is waiting for approval
🟣 Step 4: Admin approves
Admin runs:
kubectl certificate approve jane
👉 Now Kubernetes:
Signs certificate using CA
Generates final certificate
🟠 Step 5: Jane gets access
Jane receives signed certificate
Now she can run commands like:
kubectl get pods
✅ She is officially allowed into the cluster
🔄 After some time
Jane’s certificate expires
She must repeat same process
🤖 How automation helps
Instead of manual work:
Kubernetes Certificates API:
Handles CSR requests
Helps approve easily
Automates certificate handling
🧠 Easy way to remember
Think of it like:
CSR = Application form
Admin = Verifier
CA = Authority that gives ID card
Certificate = ID card to access system

**All certificates stored in controller manager**

🧠 **Kubeconfig (Super Simple Explanation)**
👉 A kubeconfig file is like a saved login + settings file for Kubernetes.
It helps you avoid typing long commands again and again.
🔐 1. Problem (Without kubeconfig)
If you want to talk to a Kubernetes cluster, you must give many details every time:
👉 Example:
kubectl get pods \
  --server https://my-kube-playground:6443 \
  --client-key admin.key \
  --client-certificate admin.crt \
  --certificate-authority ca.crt
😩 This is long and annoying!
✅ 2. Solution (Using kubeconfig)
Instead, you save all this in one file → kubeconfig
Then you just run:
kubectl get pods
🔥 Much easier!
📦 3. What’s inside kubeconfig?
It has 3 main parts:
1. Clusters 🖥️
List of Kubernetes clusters
👉 Example:
dev cluster
production cluster
2. Users 👤
Who can access the clusters
👉 Example:
admin
developer
3. Contexts 🔗 (MOST IMPORTANT)
👉 A context = cluster + user
Example:
admin using production cluster
dev using dev cluster
💡 Easy Analogy:
Think like:
Cluster = Website
User = Login account
Context = “which account logs into which website”
🔄 4. Switching between clusters
You can switch easily:
kubectl config use-context prod-user@production
👉 Example:
Before: working on dev
After: switched to production
👀 5. View current config
kubectl config view
👉 Shows:
current cluster
user
context
📂 6. Use different kubeconfig file
kubectl config view --kubeconfig=my-config
👉 Useful when:
you have multiple projects
different teams
🏷️ 7. Default Namespace
Namespace = folder inside cluster
👉 Example:
Without namespace:
kubectl get pods
Shows all pods
With default namespace:
namespace: finance
Now:
👉 kubectl get pods → shows only finance pods
🔐 8. Certificates (Security)
Kubeconfig stores security info:
👉 Two ways:
File path
certificate-authority: /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt
Direct data (encoded)
certificate-authority-data: LS0tLS1...
🎯 Final Simple Summary
kubeconfig = shortcut + saved settings
avoids typing long commands
contains:
clusters
users
contexts
lets you:
switch clusters easily
set default namespace
manage authentication

🧠 **Kubernetes API Groups — Easy Summary**
1. What is Kubernetes API?
Kubernetes API is the main way to talk to your cluster
Every command (like kubectl get pods) goes through the API server
👉 Example:
curl https://kube-master:6443/version
This returns cluster info like version, platform, etc.
2. What are API Groups?
Kubernetes organizes features into API Groups to keep things clean and manageable.
👉 Think of it like:
📁 Folder system
Each folder = one API group
Inside folder = related resources
3. Types of API Groups
🔹 A. Core API Group (Important basics)
Path: /api/v1
Contains basic Kubernetes objects
👉 Examples:
Pods
Services
Nodes
Namespaces
ConfigMaps
Secrets
👉 Example API:
/api/v1/pods
🔹 B. Named API Groups (Advanced features)
Path: /apis/<group-name>/<version>
👉 Examples:
/apis/apps → Deployments, ReplicaSets
/apis/networking.k8s.io → Network Policies
/apis/storage.k8s.io → Storage features
👉 Example:
/apis/apps/v1/deployments
4. Resources + Actions (Verbs)
Each API group has:
Resources (objects)
Verbs (actions you can perform)
👉 Common verbs:
get → read one object
list → see all objects
create → make new
delete → remove
update → modify
watch → monitor changes
👉 Example:
kubectl get pods
Resource = pods
Verb = get
5. Important API Endpoints
When you query API server, you see:
[
  "/api",
  "/api/v1",
  "/apis",
  "/healthz",
  "/metrics",
  "/logs"
]
👉 Meaning:
/api → core APIs
/apis → named APIs
/healthz → health check
/metrics → performance data
6. How to Access API Server
❌ Without authentication
curl http://localhost:6443
➡️ You get 403 Forbidden (no access)
✅ With certificates
curl http://localhost:6443 \
  --key admin.key \
  --cert admin.crt
✅ Easiest way: kubectl proxy
kubectl proxy
Then:
curl http://localhost:8001
✔ No need to manually use certificates
✔ Uses kubeconfig automatically
7. Important Note
👉 kubectl proxy ≠ kube-proxy
kubectl proxy → access API server
kube-proxy → networking inside cluster
🧾 Final Understanding
Kubernetes API = entry point to cluster
API Groups = logical grouping of features
Two main types:
Core (/api/v1)
Named (/apis/...)
Each group has:
Resources (pods, deployments)
Verbs (get, create, delete)
You can access API via:
curl (with auth)
kubectl proxy (easy way)
💡 Simple Real-Life Analogy
Think Kubernetes like a hospital system:
API Server = Reception desk
API Groups = Departments (Cardiology, Neurology)
Resources = Patients / records
Verbs = actions (check, admit, discharge)

📄 Kubernetes Authorization – Complete Master Sheet

🔐 1. Authentication vs Authorization
Concept	Meaning	Example
Authentication	Who are you?	User logs in using certificate
Authorization	What can you do?	Can user delete node?
👉 Flow:User → Authenticate → Authorize → Perform Action

👥 2. Users in Kubernetes
Type	Examples	Purpose
Humans	Admin, Developer, Tester	Manage cluster
Machines	kubelet, CI/CD bot, Jenkins	Automation
👉 As users increase → need proper permission control 

🧠 3. Central Component
👉 Kube API Server
* All requests go through it
* Handles authentication + authorization
* Communicates with:
    * Users
    * kubelets (nodes)

👥 4. ALL ROLES TOGETHER (Main Sheet)
Action	Admin	Developer	Bot
Get Pods	✅	✅	✅
Create Pods	✅	✅	✅
Delete Pods	✅	✅	❌
Get Nodes	✅	❌	❌
Delete Nodes	✅	❌	❌
Deploy Apps	✅	✅	✅
Cluster Config Change	✅	❌	❌
🧪 Example (Real Commands)
✅ Allowed

kubectl get pods

* Admin → ✅
* Developer → ✅
* Bot → ✅

❌ Forbidden

kubectl delete node worker-1

* Admin → ✅
* Developer → ❌ Forbidden
* Bot → ❌ Forbidden

❌ Error Example

Error: User "developer" cannot delete resource "nodes"


🧩 5. Authorization Types (Complete)

🔹 1. Node Authorization
Feature	Details
Used for	kubelets (nodes)
Group	system:nodes
Certificate	system:node:<node-name>
✅ Example
* Node1 → can:
    * Read its own pods
    * Update its own status
* Node1 → cannot:
    * Access Node2 data
👉 Controlled by Node Authorizer

🔹 2. ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control)
Feature	Details
Config	JSON file
Mapping	User → Permission
Update	Requires API server restart ❌
Example Policy

{"user": "dev-user", "resource": "pods", "namespace": "*"}
{"group": "dev-users", "resource": "pods"}

✅ Example Meaning
* dev-user → can manage pods
* dev-users group → same permissions
⚠️ Problem:
* Hard to maintain
* Not scalable 

🔹 3. RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) ⭐
Feature	Details
Method	Role → assign to users
Benefit	Easy, scalable
Standard	Most used
✅ Example (ALL ROLES)
🟢 Admin Role

kind: Role
metadata:
  name: admin
rules:
- resources: ["*"]
  verbs: ["*"]


🔵 Developer Role

kind: Role
metadata:
  name: developer
rules:
- resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "create", "delete"]


🟡 Bot Role

kind: Role
metadata:
  name: bot
rules:
- resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "create"]


🔗 Role Assignment

admin-user → admin
dev-user → developer
ci-bot → bot


🔁 Benefit Example
👉 Change role once → affects all users

🔹 4. Webhook / External Authorization
Feature	Details
Tool	Open Policy Agent (OPA)
Control	External system
Flow Example
1. User request
2. API server → sends to OPA
3. OPA → Allow / Deny
👉 Useful for advanced policies 

⚙️ 6. Authorization Modes

🔓 AlwaysAllow
* Allows everything

--authorization-mode=AlwaysAllow

👉 Example:
* Any user can delete nodes

🔒 AlwaysDeny
* Denies everything
👉 Example:
* Even admin cannot access pods

🔁 7. Multiple Modes (IMPORTANT)

--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC,Webhook

🔄 Flow
1. Node authorizer checks
2. If denied → RBAC checks
3. If denied → Webhook checks
4. If any approves → ✅ Allow
5. If all deny → ❌ Reject
👉 Order matters 

🧠 8. Namespace-Based Control
👉 When multiple teams share cluster:
* Dev team → only access dev namespace
* Test team → only access test namespace
✔ Prevents cross-team access

🔁 9. Full Request Flow (End-to-End)
Example:

kubectl delete node worker-2

Steps:
1. Authentication
    * User identity verified
2. Authorization
    * Node? → No
    * RBAC? → Check role
    * Webhook? → Optional
3. Result:
Role	Result
Admin	✅ Allowed
Developer	❌ Denied
Bot	❌ Denied
🧠 10. Real-Life Analogy
Kubernetes	Real Life
Authentication	Security guard checks ID
Authorization	Manager gives permission
RBAC	Job roles
Node Authorizer	Machine access control
📌 FINAL KEY POINTS
* Always use RBAC (standard)
* Avoid ABAC in real projects
* Use least privilege principle
* API Server is the core
* Multiple authorization modes can work together
* Nodes have special authorization



🔐 **Kubernetes RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) — Simple Summary**
RBAC in Kubernetes is used to control who can do what inside a cluster.
You mainly do 3 things:
1. Create a Role → define permissions
2. Create a RoleBinding → assign role to a user
3. Verify & test permissions

1️⃣ Creating a Role
A Role defines what actions are allowed on which resources.
👉 Example: A role named developer

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: developer
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["list", "get", "create", "update", "delete"]
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["ConfigMap"]
  verbs: ["create"]

✔ What this means:
* Can manage pods (list, create, delete, etc.)
* Can only create ConfigMaps
👉 Create it:

kubectl create -f developer-role.yaml

📌 Note: Roles work inside a namespace only

2️⃣ Creating a Role Binding
A RoleBinding connects a user to a role.
👉 Example: Assign role developer to user dev-user

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: devuser-developer-binding
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: dev-user
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: developer
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

👉 Create it:

kubectl create -f devuser-developer-binding.yaml

✔ Now dev-user gets all permissions from the developer role

3️⃣ Verifying Roles & Bindings
👉 Check roles:

kubectl get roles

Example output:

NAME        AGE
developer   4s

👉 Check role bindings:

kubectl get rolebindings

Example:

NAME                      AGE
devuser-developer-binding 24s

👉 Detailed info:

kubectl describe role developer
kubectl describe rolebinding devuser-developer-binding


4️⃣ Testing Permissions
Use this command:

kubectl auth can-i <action> <resource>

👉 Examples:

kubectl auth can-i create deployments
# yes

kubectl auth can-i delete nodes
# no

👉 Test for a specific user:

kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as dev-user
# no

kubectl auth can-i create pods --as dev-user
# yes

✔ Shows what a user is allowed to do

5️⃣ Restricting Access to Specific Resources
You can limit access to specific resource names
👉 Example: Only allow pods named blue and orange

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: developer
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "create", "update"]
  resourceNames: ["blue", "orange"]

✔ Now user can only access:
* pod blue
* pod orange

✅ Final Idea
* Role → defines permissions
* RoleBinding → assigns role to user
* Use kubectl auth can-i → to test permissions
* Can also restrict access to specific resources


🧾 Summary: Cluster Roles in Kubernetes
🔹 1. What problem are we solving?
In Kubernetes, permissions are controlled using RBAC (Role-Based Access Control).
Earlier:
* Roles & RoleBindings → work inside a namespace only
But:
* Some resources (like nodes) don’t belong to any namespace👉 So we need something bigger → ClusterRoles

🔹 2. Namespaced vs Cluster-scoped resources
* Namespaced resources (inside a namespace):
    * Pods
    * Services
    * Deployments
* Cluster-scoped resources (whole cluster):
    * Nodes
    * Persistent Volumes
👉 These cluster-level resources cannot use normal Roles

🔹 3. What are ClusterRoles?
* A ClusterRole gives permissions across the entire cluster
* Used for:
    * Managing nodes
    * Managing storage
    * Admin-level access

🔹 4. What are ClusterRoleBindings?
* A ClusterRoleBinding connects:
    * a user ➝ to a ClusterRole
👉 Without binding, the role does nothing

📌 Key Difference
Feature	Role	ClusterRole
Scope	One namespace	Whole cluster
Use case	Pods, services	Nodes, volumes
Access level	Limited	Global
✅ Examples
🟢 Example 1: Namespace-level Role
👉 User can only access pods in dev namespace

kind: Role
metadata:
  namespace: dev

✔ Access limited to dev only

🔵 Example 2: ClusterRole (from the page)
👉 Give permission to manage nodes (cluster-wide)

kind: ClusterRole
rules:
- resources: ["nodes"]
  verbs: ["list", "get", "create", "delete"]

✔ Works across the entire cluster 

🔵 Example 3: Binding the ClusterRole
👉 Assign that role to a user:

kind: ClusterRoleBinding
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: cluster-admin

✔ Now the user can manage nodes everywhere

🔴 Example 4: Important behavior
If a ClusterRole gives access to pods:
👉 The user can access pods in ALL namespaces
✔ Not restricted like normal roles

⚡ Final takeaway
* Use Role → when you want access inside one namespace
* Use ClusterRole → when you want access across the whole cluster
* Always remember:👉 Role = Local👉 ClusterRole = Global

**User account is meant for humans service account is meant for machines**

📘 Kubernetes Service Accounts – Complete Notes


🔹 1. What are Service Accounts?
Kubernetes has 2 types of accounts:
👤 User Accounts
* Used by humans (admin, developer)
* Example: logging into cluster, running kubectl
🤖 Service Accounts
* Used by applications / machines
* Used for API communication inside cluster
* Examples:
    * Prometheus → fetch metrics
    * Jenkins → deploy apps
    * Dashboard app → get pod info
👉 In short:
* User Account = Human
* Service Account = Application

🔹 2. Example (Simple Understanding)
A dashboard app wants to:
* Get list of Pods from Kubernetes API
* Show it on UI
➡️ It uses a Service Account token to authenticate API requests

🔹 3. Creating a Service Account
Command:

kubectl create serviceaccount dashboard-sa

List service accounts:

kubectl get serviceaccount

Output example:

NAME           SECRETS   AGE
default        1         218d
dashboard-sa   1         4d


🔹 4. Service Account Token
* When you create a service account:👉 Kubernetes automatically creates a token👉 Stored inside a Secret
Check details:

kubectl describe serviceaccount dashboard-sa

Check token:

kubectl describe secret <token-name>

👉 Token looks like:

eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsImtpZCI6...


🔹 5. How Token is Used
Used as Bearer Token for API authentication:

curl https://<kube-api> \
--header "Authorization: Bearer <TOKEN>"

👉 This allows app → talk to Kubernetes API securely

🔹 6. Automatic Token Mounting (VERY IMPORTANT ⭐)
When a Pod runs:
* Kubernetes automatically:
    * Assigns a service account
    * Mounts token inside Pod
📂 Default location:

/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount

Inside this folder:
* token
* ca.crt
* namespace

🔹 7. Default Service Account
* Every namespace has a:👉 default service account
* Every Pod uses it automatically (unless changed)

🔹 8. Example Pod (Default Service Account)

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: my-dashboard
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: my-app

👉 This Pod will use:
* default service account
* Token auto-mounted

🔹 9. Using Custom Service Account
To use your own service account:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: my-dashboard
spec:
  serviceAccountName: dashboard-sa
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: my-app

⚠️ Important:
* You cannot change service account of running Pod
* You must:
    * Delete + recreate PodOR
    * Update Deployment (auto rollout)

🔹 10. Disable Auto Mounting (Security Feature)

spec:
  automountServiceAccountToken: false

👉 Use when:
* Pod does NOT need API access
* Improves security

🔹 11. Kubernetes Version Changes 🚀
🔸 Before v1.22
* Tokens:
    * Stored in Secrets
    * No expiry ❌ (unsafe)

🔸 From v1.22 (IMPORTANT)
Introduced:👉 TokenRequest API
New tokens are:
* ⏱ Time-bound (expire)
* 🎯 Audience-bound (limited usage)
* 🔒 More secure

🔸 From v1.24
Major change:
❌ No automatic token creation in Secrets
✅ Now you must create token manually:

kubectl create token dashboard-sa

👉 Default expiry:
* ~1 hour

🔹 12. New Token (Projected Volume)
Instead of Secret, Kubernetes now uses:

volumes:
- name: kube-api-access
  projected:
    sources:
      - serviceAccountToken:
          expirationSeconds: 3607

👉 Benefits:
* Secure
* Auto-rotating tokens
* No long-lived secrets

🔹 13. Old Method (NOT Recommended ❌)
You can still create token manually via Secret:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
type: kubernetes.io/service-account-token

⚠️ But:
* No expiry
* Less secure👉 Avoid this

🔹 14. Key Exam / Interview Points ⭐
👉 Very important concepts:
* Service accounts = for applications
* Token = used for API authentication
* Default SA is auto-attached to Pods
* Token is mounted inside Pod
* Path:/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount
* Use serviceAccountName to override
* Cannot modify SA of running Pod
* v1.22 → TokenRequest API
* v1.24 → No auto secret tokens

🔹 15. Quick Summary
* Service Accounts → machine identity
* Token → access Kubernetes API
* Pods automatically get token
* You can:
    * Change service account
    * Disable token mount
* New Kubernetes versions focus on:👉 security + short-lived tokens

✅ Final Simple Explanation
👉 Think like this:
* Service Account = Login for app
* Token = Password
* Pod = App container
* Kubernetes = System

🔐 What is Security Context (quick recap)
Security Context = rules that control how a container runs(like which user, what permissions, etc.)

📦 1. Pod-Level Security Context (applies to all containers)
👉 Use this when all containers should follow same rules
✅ Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: pod-example
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsUser: 1000
  containers:
    - name: app1
      image: nginx
    - name: app2
      image: redis

💡 What happens?
* Both nginx and redis run as user ID 1000
* You don’t need to repeat config for each container

📦 2. Container-Level Security Context (more control)
👉 Use this when each container needs different settings
✅ Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: container-example
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app1
      image: nginx
      securityContext:
        runAsUser: 1000

    - name: app2
      image: redis
      securityContext:
        runAsUser: 2000

💡 What happens?
* nginx runs as user 1000
* redis runs as user 2000

⚠️ 3. Override Example (important concept)
👉 Container setting wins over pod setting
✅ Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: override-example
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsUser: 1000   # default for all

  containers:
    - name: app1
      image: nginx

    - name: app2
      image: redis
      securityContext:
        runAsUser: 2000   # overrides pod setting

💡 What happens?
* app1 → runs as 1000
* app2 → runs as 2000 (override)

🔑 4. Adding Linux Capabilities
👉 Gives extra permissions to container
✅ Example

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: capability-example
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app
      image: ubuntu
      command: ["sleep", "3600"]
      securityContext:
        capabilities:
          add: ["MAC_ADMIN"]

💡 What happens?
* Container gets special permission → MAC_ADMIN
* Useful for advanced system-level operations

🚫 5. Running as Non-Root (Best Practice)
👉 Very important for security
✅ Example

securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true

💡 Why?
* Prevents container from running as root user
* Reduces risk if container is hacked

🧠 Easy Analogy
Think of it like a company office:
* Pod-level = company rule (everyone follows)
* Container-level = individual employee rule (can override)

Kubernetes Network Policies – Easy Summary
This page explains how network policies control communication between pods in Kubernetes. 
1. Basic Traffic Flow
* Example app has:
    * Web Server
    * API Server
    * Database Server
* Flow:
    * User → Web Server (port 80)
    * Web → API (port 5000)
    * API → Database (port 3306)
2. Types of Traffic
* Ingress = Incoming traffic to a pod
* Egress = Outgoing traffic from a pod
3. Default Kubernetes Behavior
* All pods can talk to each other freely
* This is called "all-allow" communication
4. Why Network Policies?
* To restrict communication for security
* Example:
    * Web server should NOT talk directly to database
    * Only API server can access database
5. What is a Network Policy?
* A Kubernetes object that controls traffic
* Applied using:
    * Labels
    * Selectors
6. Example Rule
* Allow only:
    * API pod → Database pod
    * On port 3306
7. Sample YAML

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: db-policy
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      role: db
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          name: api-pod
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 3306

8. Key Points
* podSelector → selects which pod policy applies to
* policyTypes → defines traffic type (Ingress/Egress)
* Only defined traffic is restricted; others may still be allowed
9. Apply Policy

kubectl create -f db-policy.yaml

10. Important Note
* Network policies work only if supported by your network plugin
* Supported: Calico, Weave, Kube-router, Romana
* Not supported: Flannel

Final Idea
* By default → everything is open
* With network policies → you control who can talk to whom


🧠 Summary (in simple words)
This page explains Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs) in Kubernetes.
1. How Kubernetes normally works
* Kubernetes has built-in resources like Deployment, Pod, etc.
* Each resource has a controller that ensures things run as expected.
* Example:If you say “I want 3 pods”, Kubernetes automatically creates and maintains 3 pods.
👉 This is called desired state → actual state matching.

2. What are Custom Resources?
* You can create your own resource type (not built-in).
* Example: FlightTicket
* You can create, list, delete it using kubectl.
BUT ⚠️By default, it does nothing — it’s just stored in Kubernetes (etcd). 

3. Why CRD is needed
If you try to create a custom resource without defining it:
❌ Kubernetes gives error:
"no matches for kind FlightTicket"
So you must define it using a CRD.

4. What does a CRD do?
A CRD tells Kubernetes:
* This new resource exists
* Its name, version, structure
* What fields it has (like from, to, number)
After creating CRD:✅ Kubernetes can understand your custom object

5. Why Custom Controller is needed
Even after CRD:
* Kubernetes just stores data
* It does NOT perform any action
So you need a custom controller to:
* Watch your resource
* Take action (like calling APIs)

✈️ Simple Example
Step 1: Create Custom Resource (FlightTicket)

apiVersion: flights.com/v1
kind: FlightTicket
metadata:
  name: my-flight-ticket
spec:
  from: Mumbai
  to: London
  number: 2

👉 This means:
* Book 2 tickets from Mumbai → London

Step 2: What happens without controller?
* Kubernetes stores this object
* Nothing else happens ❌
* No ticket is booked

Step 3: Add Custom Controller
Now controller logic:
* Watches FlightTicket
* Calls booking API
👉 When you create object:
* Controller sees it
* Calls API → ticket booked ✅

🔁 Real-Life Analogy
Think of it like:
* CRD = Form template
* Custom Resource = Filled form
* Controller = Person processing the form
Without the person → nothing happens.

🧾 Final Key Points
* Kubernetes built-in resources → already have controllers
* Custom resources → need:
    1. CRD → to define them
    2. Controller → to make them work


🔹 What is this page about?
This page explains how to build a custom controller in Kubernetes.
👉 A controller is a program that:
* Continuously watches your cluster
* Takes action when something changes

🔹 Key Idea (Very Important)
You first create a Custom Resource (like FlightTicket)Then you build a controller that watches it and performs actions.

🔹 Example: FlightTicket Resource
You define a custom object like this:

apiVersion: flights.com/v1
kind: FlightTicket
metadata:
  name: my-flight-ticket
spec:
  from: Mumbai
  to: London
  number: 2

👉 This means:
* Book 2 tickets from Mumbai → London

🔹 What happens next?
When you create it:

kubectl create -f flightticket.yml

Kubernetes stores it, but nothing happens automatically yet.
👉 Status:

Pending


🔹 Role of the Controller
The controller watches this object and reacts.
Example:
When it sees:

FlightTicket created

It can:
* Call a flight booking API
* Book tickets
* Update status
👉 So the controller makes things actually happen

🔹 Simple Analogy
* FlightTicket (CRD) = Order placed
* Controller = Worker processing the order

🔹 Why use Go (instead of Python)?
Because Go + Kubernetes client provides:
* ✅ Built-in caching
* ✅ Queue system
* ✅ Better performance

🔹 How to build the controller
Steps:
1. Clone sample project:

git clone https://github.com/kubernetes/sample-controller.git

1. Add your logic in:

controller.go

Example function:

callBookFlightAPI()

1. Build it:

go build -o sample-controller .

1. Run it:

./sample-controller -kubeconfig=$HOME/.kube/config


🔹 What the controller does internally
* Watches FlightTicket objects
* Detects:
    * Create
    * Update
* Calls your logic (like booking API)

🔹 Deployment
Instead of running manually:
* Package it as a Docker image
* Run it as a Pod or Deployment
👉 This is how it runs in production

🔹 Final Summary
* You create a custom resource (FlightTicket)
* Controller:
    * Watches it
    * Takes action (like booking tickets)
* Built using Go for efficiency
* Can be deployed inside Kubernetes

🔹 One-line understanding
👉 CRD = What you want👉 Controller = Makes it happen

  📘 Summary: Operator Framework (Kubernetes)
The Operator Framework is a way to automate and simplify managing applications in Kubernetes.
👉 Problem before Operators
Earlier, you had to:
1. Create a CRD (Custom Resource Definition)
2. Create a custom controller
3. Deploy both separately
This was manual and complex.

✅ What Operators Do
An Operator combines everything into one package:
* Creates the CRD
* Deploys the controller
* Manages the application automatically
👉 So instead of doing many steps manually, you just deploy one operator.

✈️ Example: Flight Operator
When you deploy a flight operator:
* It creates a CRD called FlightTicket
* It runs a controller that watches these tickets
* It automatically performs actions (like booking flights)
Example CRD

kind: CustomResourceDefinition
metadata:
  name: flighttickets.flights.com

What happens:
* You create a resource like:kind: FlightTicket
* The operator:👉 Detects it👉 Calls booking API👉 Manages the process

⚙️ Real Example: etcd Operator
A popular real-world operator is the etcd operator.
It can:
* Deploy an etcd cluster
* Monitor it
* Take backups
* Restore data if something fails
👉 You don’t need to manually manage etcd anymore.

🔁 What Tasks Operators Automate
Operators replace manual admin work like:
* App installation
* Updates
* Backups
* Disaster recovery
* Troubleshooting
👉 Basically, they act like an automated system administrator.

🚀 How to Use an Operator
Simple steps:
1. Install Operator Lifecycle Manager (OLM)
2. Install the operator
3. Start using it
Example:

kubectl create -f etcd.yaml

🧠 Docker Storage — Easy Summary
1. Where Docker stores data
* Docker keeps everything inside: /var/lib/docker
* Important folders:
    * images → stores images
    * containers → running container data
    * volumes → saved data
👉 Think of it like:
* Images = templates
* Containers = running apps
* Volumes = saved data

2. Docker Image Layers (Very Important)
Docker images are built in layers.
Each line in Dockerfile = one layer.
Example:

FROM ubuntu
RUN install python
COPY app code

👉 Layers:
1. Ubuntu base
2. Install Python
3. Copy app code
💡 Key idea:
* Docker reuses layers if nothing changes
* Saves time + storage
Example:
* App1 and App2 both use Ubuntu + Python
* Docker reuses those layers
* Only new code layer is added
👉 Result:
* Faster builds 🚀
* Less disk usage

3. Container Writable Layer (Copy-on-Write)
When you run a container:
* Docker adds a writable layer on top
Example:

docker run my-app

Now:
* Original image = read-only
* New layer = writable
Example:
* You edit a file inside container
* Docker:
    1. Copies file from image
    2. Changes it in writable layer
👉 This is called Copy-On-Write
💡 Important:
* When container is deleted → data is LOST

4. Problem: Data gets deleted 😬
Because container storage is temporary.
👉 Solution: Volumes & Bind Mounts

5. Volumes (Best for persistence)
Docker-managed storage.
Example:

docker volume create mydata
docker run -v mydata:/data mysql

👉 Data stays even if container is deleted

6. Bind Mounts (Use your own folder)
Use your system folder directly.
Example:

docker run -v /myfolder:/data mysql

👉 Now:
* /myfolder (your PC) = /data (container)

7. Storage Drivers (Behind the scenes)
These manage how Docker handles storage.
Examples:
* AUFS
* Overlay2 (most common)
* ZFS
* BTRFS
👉 You don’t usually change this manuallyDocker selects best one automatically

📌 Final Easy Analogy
Think of Docker like this:
* Image = Recipe 📖
* Layers = Steps in recipe
* Container = Cooked dish 🍲
* Writable layer = Plate where you modify food
* Volume = Fridge (stores food safely)

🔹 What this page is about
It explains volume driver plugins in Docker, which are used to manage persistent storage (data that stays even after containers stop).

🔹 Key ideas
1. Volumes vs Storage Drivers
* Storage drivers → manage container/image storage (internal working)
* Volumes → used for saving data permanently
* Volumes are NOT handled by storage drivers
* Instead, they are handled by volume driver plugins

2. Default Volume Driver
* Docker uses a default plugin called local driver
* It stores data on your machine at:/var/lib/docker/volumes

3. Other Volume Driver Plugins
You can use plugins to store data on external/cloud storage like:
* Azure File Storage
* AWS (EBS, S3)
* Google Persistent Disk
* NetApp
* VMware vSphere
* Portworx, RexRay, etc.
👉 This is useful when you want:
* Data to survive container deletion
* Data stored in cloud or shared systems

4. Example (Important)
You can run a container and choose a specific storage plugin:

docker run -it \
  --name mysql \
  --volume-driver rexray/ebs \
  --mount src=ebs-vol,target=/var/lib/mysql \
  mysql

✔ This creates storage in AWS EBS instead of your local machine✔ Data stays safe even if the container stops

5. Tip
* Choose a volume driver based on your infrastructure needs
* Example: RexRay supports multiple cloud providers → very flexible

📌 What is CSI (Container Storage Interface)?
* CSI is a standard way for container platforms (like Kubernetes) to connect with different storage systems.
* It removes the need to write storage code inside the main system.
👉 In short:CSI = a common “language” between containers and storage systems

🔄 Why was CSI created?
Earlier:
* Kubernetes only worked with Docker directly (hardcoded).
* Supporting new tools required changing Kubernetes code.
Then:
* CRI → solved runtime problem (Docker, CRI-O, etc.)
* CNI → solved networking problem
👉 Similarly, CSI solves the storage problem by standardizing it. 

⚙️ What does CSI do?
* Lets storage companies create plugins (drivers).
* These drivers work with Kubernetes or other platforms.
Examples of storage systems:
* Amazon EBS
* Azure Disk
* NetApp
* Dell EMC
* Nutanix
👉 So, Kubernetes doesn’t care which storage you use — as long as it supports CSI. 

🧠 How CSI works (simple flow)
1. You create a pod that needs storage
2. Kubernetes sends a request:➝ “Create volume”
3. CSI driver:
    * Creates storage in backend (like AWS, NetApp, etc.)
    * Sends response back
4. When done:
    * Kubernetes sends “Delete volume” request
👉 All communication happens through standard RPC calls

💡 Real-Life Example
Imagine:
* You run an app in Kubernetes that needs a database.
* The database needs storage.
Without CSI:
* Kubernetes must know how to talk to AWS, Azure, etc. → complicated.
With CSI:
* Kubernetes just says:👉 “I need storage”
* CSI driver handles the rest.
Example:
* Pod requests storage
* CSI driver creates an Amazon EBS volume
* Pod uses it

🧾 Key Takeaways
* CSI is a standard interface for storage
* Works across multiple platforms (not just Kubernetes)
* Makes systems:
    * Flexible
    * Scalable
    * Easy to extend

🧠 Kubernetes Volumes — Simple Explanation
🔹 1. Why Volumes Are Needed
👉 Problem
* Containers (in Docker) and Pods (in Kubernetes) are temporary (ephemeral)
* When they stop or are deleted → all data inside is lost
👉 Solution
* Use Volumes to store data outside the container/pod
* So data remains even after deletion
📌 Same idea in both:
* Docker → Volumes
* Kubernetes → Volumes

🔹 2. Docker vs Kubernetes Volumes
Feature	Docker	Kubernetes
Nature	Containers are temporary	Pods are temporary
Problem	Data lost when container stops	Data lost when pod is deleted
Solution	Attach Docker volume	Attach Kubernetes volume
👉 Concept is the same, only implementation differs 

🔹 3. How Kubernetes Volumes Work
* A volume is attached to a pod
* The pod writes data to the volume
* Even if the pod is deleted → data stays in the volume

🔹 4. Simple Example (Very Important)
💡 Scenario
* A pod generates a random number
* Saves it in file: /opt/number.out
❌ Without Volume
* Pod deleted → file gone ❌
✅ With Volume
* File saved in volume → persists ✅

🔧 YAML Example (Simplified Explanation)

volumes:
- name: data-volume
  hostPath:
    path: /data


volumeMounts:
- mountPath: /opt
  name: data-volume


🧩 What’s Happening Here?
Inside Container	On Host Machine
/opt folder	/data folder
👉 They are linked (mounted)
So:
* Writing to /opt/number.out inside container➡️ actually writes to /data/number.out on host
📌 Even if pod is deleted → file still exists on host 

🔹 5. hostPath Volume (Important Concept)
✅ What it is:
* Uses a folder from the node (machine)
👍 Good for:
* Single-node clusters
* Testing / learning
❌ Problem:
* Not good for multi-node clusters(because each node has its own /data folder)

🔹 6. Storage Options in Kubernetes
For real-world production, Kubernetes supports many storage systems:
🗂️ Common Options
Storage	Use Case
hostPath	Single node
NFS	Shared storage
AWS EBS	AWS cloud
Azure Disk	Azure cloud
Google PD	GCP cloud
CephFS / GlusterFS	Distributed storage
👉 These allow:
* Data sharing across nodes
* Better reliability and scaling 

🔹 7. Example (Real-Life Analogy)
🏠 Without Volume
* You write notes on a whiteboard in a rented room
* When you leave → everything erased ❌
💾 With Volume
* You store notes in a notebook
* Even if you leave the room → notes stay with you ✅

🔹 8. Key Takeaways (Super Important)
* Pods/containers are temporary
* Volumes provide data persistence
* hostPath:
    * Easy but only for single-node
* Production systems use:
    * Cloud storage or distributed storage

🔹 9. One-Line Summary
👉 Volumes in Kubernetes allow pods to store data permanently, even after they are deleted.


📘 Topic: Persistent Volumes (PVs) in Kubernetes
This lesson explains how storage is managed in Kubernetes using Persistent Volumes (PVs).

🔴 Problem (Before PVs)
* Earlier, storage was defined inside each Pod YAML file.
* Example: Every pod had its own volume config.
* ❌ Issues:
    * Repetition (same config in many pods)
    * Hard to manage and update
    * Not scalable in large environments

🟢 Solution: Persistent Volumes (PVs)
* A Persistent Volume (PV) is a central storage resource created by an administrator.
* Instead of defining storage in every pod:
    * Storage is created once (PV)
    * Users request storage using Persistent Volume Claims (PVCs)
👉 So:
* Admin → creates PV
* User/App → uses PVC to access PV

⚙️ How PV Works (Concept)
* PV = actual storage (cluster-level)
* PVC = request for storage
* Pods use PVC → which connects to PV

🛠️ Creating a Persistent Volume
Steps:
1. Define YAML with:
    * apiVersion: v1
    * kind: PersistentVolume
    * metadata.name
2. Set access modes:
    * ReadWriteOnce → one node can read/write
    * ReadOnlyMany → many nodes read only
    * ReadWriteMany → many nodes read/write
3. Set storage capacity:
    * Example: 1Gi
4. Choose storage type:
    * Example used: hostPath (local node storage)

📄 Example PV YAML

apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: pv-vol1
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  capacity:
    storage: 1Gi
  hostPath:
    path: /tmp/data


▶️ Commands
* Create PV:kubectl create -f pv.yaml
* Check PV:kubectl get persistentvolume

⚠️ Important Notes
* hostPath is:
    * ✅ Good for testing
    * ❌ Not recommended for production
* In production, use real storage like:
    * AWS Elastic Block Store (EBS)

🔚 Conclusion
* PVs help centralize storage management
* They remove duplication and improve scalability
* Next step: learn Persistent Volume Claims (PVCs)


📌 Persistent Volume Claims (PVC) – Quick Summary
* PVC (Persistent Volume Claim) is a request for storage in Kubernetes. 
* PV (Persistent Volume) is the actual storage created by an admin. 
👉 So basically:
* Admin creates PV
* User creates PVC
* Kubernetes connects (binds) them automatically

🔗 How Binding Works
* When you create a PVC, Kubernetes finds a matching PV based on:
    * Storage size
    * Access mode (like ReadWriteOnce)
    * Storage class
* If multiple PVs match → labels/selectors can choose one
* If no match → PVC stays Pending
⚠️ Important:
* If PVC asks for less storage than PV → extra space is wasted (not shared)

🛠️ Creating a PVC
Steps:
1. Write a YAML file with:
    * Name
    * Access mode
    * Storage size (e.g., 500Mi)
2. Run:kubectl create -f pvc-definition.yaml
3. Check status:kubectl get persistentvolumeclaim
* Initially → Pending
* After matching PV → Bound

🗑️ Deleting PVC & Reclaim Policies
When PVC is deleted, what happens to PV depends on Reclaim Policy:
Policy	What happens
Retain	PV stays, admin must clean it manually
Delete	PV is deleted automatically
Recycle	Data wiped and reused (deprecated)
🧠 Key Takeaways
* PVC = request, PV = actual storage
* Kubernetes auto-matches them
* PVC stays pending if no suitable PV
* Reclaim policy controls what happens after deletion

📦 Kubernetes Storage Classes — Quick Summary
🔹 What is this about?
This page explains how Kubernetes manages storage for applications using Storage Classes, making storage setup easier and automatic.

🧱 1. Static Provisioning (Manual way)
* You manually create storage (like a disk in Google Cloud).
* Then create:
    * PV (PersistentVolume) → actual storage
    * PVC (PersistentVolumeClaim) → request for storage
* Finally, attach it to a Pod.
👉 Problem:
* Time-consuming
* Manual work every time
* Not scalable

⚡ 2. Dynamic Provisioning (Automatic way)
* You create a Storage Class with a provisioner.
* When a PVC is created, Kubernetes:
    * Automatically creates storage (disk)
    * Creates a PV
    * Binds it to the PVC
👉 Benefits:
* No manual disk creation
* Faster and scalable
* Less chance of errors

⚙️ 3. Storage Classes (Key Idea)
A Storage Class defines:
* How storage is created
* What type of storage to use
Example:
* Standard disk
* SSD disk
* Regional (replicated) disk

🏷️ 4. Custom Storage Types
You can define different levels like:
* Silver → standard disk (cheap, basic)
* Gold → SSD (faster)
* Platinum → SSD + replication (high reliability)
👉 Apps choose storage by selecting the storage class in PVC.

🧠 Final Takeaway
* Static provisioning = manual, slow
* Dynamic provisioning (using Storage Classes) = automatic, efficient
* Storage Classes help match performance + cost + reliability to your app needs

📘 Summary: Kubernetes Networking Prerequisites

This page explains basic Linux networking concepts that are important before learning Kubernetes networking.
It mainly covers:
* Switching (same network communication)
* Routing (different network communication)
* Gateways (default path to outside networks)
* Linux router setup
* IP forwarding

🔹 1. Switching (Same Network Communication)
👉 Devices in the same network talk using a switch.
Example:
* PC A → 192.168.1.10
* PC B → 192.168.1.11
* Both connected to a switch
They can directly communicate:

ping 192.168.1.11

✔ Works because both are in the same network (192.168.1.0)

🔹 2. Routing (Different Networks)
👉 If devices are in different networks, they need a router.
Example:
* Network 1 → 192.168.1.0
* Network 2 → 192.168.2.0
Router:
* 192.168.1.1 (for network 1)
* 192.168.2.1 (for network 2)
How communication works:
* PC A (192.168.1.10) wants to reach PC C (192.168.2.10)
* It sends data to router (192.168.1.1)
* Router forwards it to other network
Command example:

ip route add 192.168.2.0/24 via 192.168.1.1

✔ This tells system:“Send traffic for 192.168.2.x through router”

🔹 3. Gateway (Internet Access)
👉 Gateway = default path to outside networks (like internet)
Example:

ip route add default via 192.168.2.1

✔ Meaning:
* If destination is unknown → send to this router

🔹 4. Multiple Routes Example
👉 You can define different routes for different networks.
Example:

ip route add 192.168.1.0/24 via 192.168.2.2

✔ So:
* Internal traffic → specific router
* Other traffic → default gateway

🔹 5. Linux as a Router
👉 A Linux machine can act like a router.
Example setup:
* Host A → 192.168.1.5
* Host B → 192.168.1.6 + 192.168.2.6 (acts as router)
* Host C → 192.168.2.5
Steps:
On Host A:

ip route add 192.168.2.0/24 via 192.168.1.6

On Host C:

ip route add 192.168.1.0/24 via 192.168.2.6

✔ Now A and C can communicate through B

🔹 6. IP Forwarding (Important!)
👉 By default, Linux does NOT forward traffic between networks.
Enable it:

echo 1 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward

✔ Without this → routing won’t work
To make permanent:

net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1


🔹 7. Important Commands (Quick View)
Task	Command
List interfaces	ip link
Show IP	ip addr
Add IP	ip addr add ...
View routes	route
Add route	ip route add ...
Default gateway	ip route add default ...

🧠 DNS Basics (Easy Summary)
1. What is DNS?
DNS = Domain Name System👉 It converts names → IP addresses
* Humans use names → google.com
* Computers use numbers → 142.250.183.14
👉 DNS connects both.

2. Local Name Resolution (/etc/hosts)
💡 Idea:
You manually tell your computer:
“This name = this IP”
✅ Example:

192.168.1.11    db

Now:

ping db

✔ Works (because system knows db → IP)

⚠️ Important:
* You can even fake names:

192.168.1.11    www.google.com

👉 Your system will think Google is your local machine 😄

❌ Problem:
* Works only for small networks
* Hard to manage for many computers

3. Centralized DNS Server
💡 Idea:
Instead of editing every system → use one DNS server
✅ Setup:

/etc/resolv.conf
nameserver 192.168.1.100

Now:
* System asks DNS server for IP

🔁 Order of checking:
From /etc/nsswitch.conf:

hosts: files dns

👉 Meaning:
1. Check /etc/hosts
2. Then DNS server

❌ If not found:

ping www.facebook.com

👉 ❌ Fails if DNS not configured properly

4. Domain Name Structure
Example: www.google.com
Breakdown:
* .com → Top-level domain (TLD)
* google → Main domain
* www → Subdomain

✅ Example:
* mail.google.com → email service
* maps.google.com → maps
👉 Subdomains = different services

5. Search Domains (Short Names)
💡 Idea:
Use short names instead of full names
✅ Setup:

search mycompany.com

Now:

ping web

👉 Automatically becomes:

web.mycompany.com


✅ Multiple domains:

search mycompany.com prod.mycompany.com

System tries:
1. web.mycompany.com
2. web.prod.mycompany.com

6. DNS Record Types
Type	Use
A	Name → IPv4
AAAA	Name → IPv6
CNAME	Alias name
✅ Examples:
* A record:web → 192.168.1.1
* CNAME:food → web

7. DNS Tools
🔹 ping
* Basic check

🔹 nslookup

nslookup google.com

✔ Talks only to DNS server❌ Ignores /etc/hosts

🔹 dig

dig google.com

✔ Detailed DNS info✔ Used for troubleshooting

🔑 Key Takeaways (Super Simple)
* DNS = name → IP translator
* /etc/hosts = manual mapping
* DNS server = centralized mapping
* System checks:
    1. hosts file
    2. DNS server
* Domains = structured (subdomain.domain.TLD)
* Tools: ping, nslookup, dig

🧠 Network Namespaces (Simple Explanation)
🏠 What is a Network Namespace?
Think of your computer like a big house, and namespaces are separate rooms.
* Each room (namespace) is private
* People in one room cannot see others directly
* But the house owner (host system) can see everything
👉 In Linux, a network namespace gives each container its own network world

📦 Example (Container vs Host)
Inside a container:
* You only see your own process (like nginx)
On the host:
* You see all processes, including containers
👉 So:
* Container = limited view
* Host = full view

🌐 Network Isolation (Very Important)
Each namespace has its own:
* Network interfaces
* IP addresses
* Routing table
* ARP table
👉 Meaning:
* A container cannot see host network
* Host network is hidden
Example:
* Host has eth0
* Container only sees lo (loopback)

🔌 How Do Namespaces Talk to Each Other?
By default ❌ they cannot communicate
Solution → Use veth pair (virtual cable)
Think:
* Like a wire connecting two rooms
Example:
* Namespace RED → IP: 192.168.15.1
* Namespace BLUE → IP: 192.168.15.2
Now they can:
* Ping each other ✅

🔁 Problem with Many Namespaces
If you connect many namespaces:
* Too many cables 😵
Solution → Use a Bridge (Virtual Switch)
Think:
* Like a Wi-Fi router inside the house
All namespaces connect to it instead of each other
Example:
* Bridge: v-net-0
* All namespaces connect to this bridge
* Now all can communicate easily

🧑‍💻 Host Communication
To allow host to talk to namespaces:
* Give bridge an IP (e.g., 192.168.15.5)
Now:
* Host can ping containers ✅

🌍 External Network Access (Internet)
Problem:
* Containers use private IP → not visible outside
Step 1: Add route
Tell container:👉 "Send external traffic via host"
Step 2: Use NAT (very important)
* Host changes container IP → its own IP
* So external systems can reply
👉 This is how containers access:
* Internet
* Other networks

📡 Inbound Access (Outside → Container)
By default ❌ not possible
Two solutions:
1. Static Route
* Configure router to reach container network
2. Port Forwarding (Most Common ✅)
Example:
* Host port 80 → Container port 80
👉 So users access:

host-ip:80 → container app


🔑 Key Takeaways (Super Simple)
* Namespace = isolated network space
* Containers use namespaces for security & separation
* Communication needs:
    * veth pair (for 2 namespaces)
    * bridge (for many)
* Internet access needs:
    * routing + NAT
* External access needs:
    * port forwarding

