🧠 How to Read This Diagram
* RED & BLUE → separate network namespaces (containers)
* Each has its own:
    * IP
    * Interface (veth)
* Bridge (v-net-0) → acts like a switch
* Host:
    * connects everything
    * does NAT for internet access

💡 If Mermaid doesn't work
Here’s a simple ASCII fallback:

        [ Container RED ]        [ Container BLUE ]
        192.168.15.1             192.168.15.2
              |                         |
          veth-red                 veth-blue
              \                       /
               \                     /
                \                   /
                 [ Bridge v-net-0 ]
                     192.168.15.5
                           |
                        [ Host ]
                           |
                        (NAT)
                           |
                    [ Internet / LAN ]

🐳 Docker Networking – Complete Notes

🧠 1. Basic Idea
* Docker runs containers inside a host machine
* Each container needs networking to:
    * talk to other containers
    * talk to internet
    * be accessed from outside
👉 Networking is controlled by network modes

🔌 2. Docker Network Modes
🚫 A. NONE Network
📌 Concept
* No network connection at all
❌ Features
* No internet
* No communication with other containers
* Fully isolated

📊 Diagram






💻 Command

docker run --network none nginx


🧠 Example
👉 Like a room with no doors/windows 🔒

🖥️ B. HOST Network
📌 Concept
* Container shares host network
✅ Features
* Same IP as host
* No isolation
* Direct access
⚠️ Limitation
* Cannot run multiple apps on same port

📊 Diagram






💻 Command

docker run --network host nginx


🧠 Example
👉 Container uses host’s port directly

🌉 C. BRIDGE Network (Default)
📌 Concept
* Docker creates internal private network
* Name: bridge (docker0)
📊 Details
* Subnet: 172.17.0.0/16
* Bridge IP: 172.17.0.1

✅ Features
* Each container gets its own IP
* Containers can communicate
* External access requires port mapping

📊 Diagram






💻 Commands

docker run nginx
docker run nginx


🧠 Example
👉 Like private society 🏘️ (internal communication allowed)

🔧 3. How Bridge Network Works Internally

📌 Steps
1. Create network namespace
2. Create veth pair (virtual cable)
3. Connect:
    * one end → container
    * other end → docker0
4. Assign IP

📊 Diagram






📌 Container Interface
* Inside container → eth0
* Example IP:

172.17.0.3


🌍 4. Port Mapping (VERY IMPORTANT)

❓ Problem
* Containers are private
* Cannot access from outside

✅ Solution
👉 Port Mapping (Publishing)

💻 Command

docker run -p 8080:80 nginx


📌 Meaning
Host Port	Container Port
8080	80
📊 Diagram






🧠 Flow

User → Host:8080 → Container:80


❌ Without Port Mapping

curl http://172.17.0.3:80

👉 May fail

✅ With Port Mapping

curl http://192.168.1.10:8080

👉 Works ✅

⚙️ 5. Behind the Scene (iptables)
Docker uses NAT rules

📌 Rule Example

Host:8080 → Container:80


📊 Diagram






🧾 6. Network Commands

List networks

docker network ls


Inspect container

docker inspect <container_id>


Check interfaces

ip link show


Check namespace

ip netns list


📊 7. Quick Comparison Table
Mode	Internet	Container Communication	External Access	Use Case
NONE	❌	❌	❌	Isolation
HOST	✅	✅	✅	Performance
BRIDGE	✅	✅	❌ (needs mapping)	Default
🧠 8. Real-Life Analogy
Mode	Example
NONE	Locked room 🔒
HOST	Shared entrance 🚪
BRIDGE	Gated society 🏘️
