🧠 Summary: Container Networking Interface (CNI)
🔹 What problem does CNI solve?
Containers need networking (IP, communication, internet access).
Every platform (Docker, Kubernetes, etc.) used to configure networking in its own way → messy and repetitive.
CNI standardizes this process so everything works consistently.
🔹 Core Idea
Each container runs in its own network namespace (isolated network).
A bridge network connects containers (like a virtual switch).
Instead of each platform doing networking manually, they call a CNI plugin.
🔹 What does CNI define?
CNI is a standard (rules) for plugins.
A plugin must:
Handle commands:
ADD → setup network
DEL → remove network
CHECK → verify network
Use inputs like:
Container ID
Network namespace
Configure:
IP address
Routing
Connectivity
🔹 How it works (flow)
Container is created
Runtime (like Kubernetes) calls CNI plugin
Plugin:
creates network
assigns IP
connects to bridge
Container is ready to communicate
🔹 Key Benefit
👉 Any CNI plugin works with any runtime (if both follow standard)
🔹 Examples of CNI Plugins
Bridge
VLAN / MACVLAN / IPVLAN
Calico
Flannel
Cilium
Weave
🔹 Important Note
Docker uses CNM (not CNI)
To use CNI with Docker:
docker run --network=none nginx
bridge add <container-id> <namespace>
📊 Diagram (Copy-Paste Friendly)
✅ Simple Flow Diagram
+-------------------+
| Container Runtime |
| (Kubernetes)      |
+---------+---------+
          |
          | calls
          v
+-------------------+
|   CNI Plugin      |
| (bridge/calico)   |
+---------+---------+
          |
          | configures
          v
+------------------------+
| Network Namespace      |
| (per container)        |
+---------+--------------+
          |
          | connected via
          v
+------------------------+
| Bridge Network         |
| (virtual switch)       |
+---------+--------------+
          |
          v
+------------------------+
| Other Containers / Net |
+------------------------+
