🌐 What is Cluster Networking?
This page explains how networking works in a Kubernetes cluster.
Basic requirements:
* Every node (master + worker) must have:
    * At least one network interface
    * A valid IP address
* Each machine must have:
    * Unique hostname
    * Unique MAC address
    * (Very important when cloning VMs)

🔐 Required Ports in Kubernetes
For all components to communicate properly, some ports must be open:
Port	Used By	Purpose
6443	API Server (master)	Main communication point for cluster
10250	Kubelet (all nodes)	Node monitoring & management
10259	Scheduler (master)	Assigns pods to nodes
10257	Controller Manager (master)	Maintains cluster state
30000–32767	Worker nodes	External access to services
2379	etcd	Client communication
2380	etcd	Communication between etcd servers
💡 These ports must be open in:
* Firewalls
* Cloud security groups (AWS, Azure, GCP)

🧪 Verifying Network Setup
You can check your network using these commands:
🔍 View interfaces

ip link

📡 View IP addresses

ip addr

➕ Assign IP manually

ip addr add 192.168.1.10/24 dev eth0

🛣️ Check routing

ip route

➕ Add route

ip route add 192.168.1.0/24 via 192.168.2.1

🔁 Check IP forwarding

cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward

🔗 ARP table

arp

🔌 Check open ports/services

netstat -plnt

👉 These help in:
* Troubleshooting network issues
* Verifying connectivity
* Checking port usage



🧠 Pod Networking in Kubernetes — Easy Summary
🔹 What is Pod Networking?
* In Kubernetes, every pod gets its own unique IP address.
* Pods can communicate:
    * With other pods on the same node
    * With pods on different nodes
* This works without using NAT, making communication simple and direct.

🔹 How Networking Works (Basic Idea)
* Kubernetes uses:
    * Network namespaces → isolate each pod’s network
    * Bridge networks → connect pods on the same node
    * veth pairs (virtual cables) → link pods to the bridge
👉 Think of it like:
* Each pod = a small computer
* Bridge = a local switch
* veth pair = cable connecting them

🔹 Cluster Network Setup
* Each node has:
    * Its own external IP (e.g., 192.168.1.x)
    * Its own pod subnet (e.g., 10.244.x.0/24)
Example:
* Node 1 → 10.244.1.0/24
* Node 2 → 10.244.2.0/24
* Node 3 → 10.244.3.0/24
When a pod is created:
1. It gets an IP from the node’s subnet
2. It connects to the bridge
3. Routes are set so it can communicate

🔹 Problem: Cross-Node Communication
* Pods on different nodes can’t talk by default
* You must add routing rules so nodes know:
    * “Where to send traffic for other pod networks”
Example:
* Node 1 sends traffic for Node 2 pods via Node 2’s IP
⚠️ Manual routing is:
* Fine for learning
* Not practical for real systems

🔹 Solution: CNI (Container Network Interface)
* Container Network Interface (CNI) automates networking
CNI does:
* Runs scripts when pods are created
* Assigns IP addresses
* Connects pods to the network
* Removes network when pod is deleted
👉 It has:
* Add → connect pod
* Delete → clean up

🔹 Key Takeaways
* Every pod has a unique IP
* Pods can communicate across nodes
* Networking uses bridges + virtual interfaces
* Routing is needed for cross-node traffic
* CNI automates everything in real clusters

📌 Final Idea
This page explains:
* How pod networking works from scratch
* And why tools like CNI are needed for real-world Kubernetes


📘 CNI in Kubernetes – Complete Notes
🔹 1. What is CNI?
* CNI (Container Network Interface) is used by Kubernetes to connect containers to a network. 
* It defines how networking should be done for containers.
👉 Simple:
* CNI = rules + plugins to give network to containers

🔹 2. How Kubernetes Uses CNI
When a container is created:
1. Container runtime (like containerd / CRI-O) creates container
2. It creates a network namespace
3. Calls CNI plugin
4. Plugin connects container to network
👉 Flow:

Container → Runtime → CNI Plugin → Network Connected

📌 Important:
* Docker is no longer main runtime
* Now mostly containerd is used 

🔹 3. Important Directories
Directory	Purpose
/opt/cni/bin	Contains CNI plugins
/etc/cni/net.d	Contains network config files
👉 Runtime:
* Looks for plugins in /opt/cni/bin
* Reads config from /etc/cni/net.d

🔹 4. Example: List of CNI Plugins

ls /opt/cni/bin

👉 Output example:

bridge  flannel  host-local  macvlan  ptp ...

✅ Meaning:
* bridge → basic networking
* flannel → cluster networking
* host-local → IP allocation
👉 Simple:This is like a toolbox of networking options

🔹 5. Example: Config File Selection

ls /etc/cni/net.d

👉 Output:

10-bridge.conflist

✅ Meaning:
* Kubernetes picks first file alphabetically
* Here → bridge network is used
👉 Simple:
* This file tells:👉 “Which network plugin to use”

🔹 6. Example: Bridge Configuration File

cat /etc/cni/net.d/10-bridge.conf


{
  "cniVersion": "0.2.0",
  "name": "mynet",
  "type": "bridge",
  "bridge": "cni0",
  "isGateway": true,
  "ipMasq": true,
  "ipam": {
    "type": "host-local",
    "subnet": "10.22.0.0/16",
    "routes": [
      { "dst": "0.0.0.0/0" }
    ]
  }
}


🔹 7. Explanation of Fields (Very Easy)
Field	Meaning
name	Network name (mynet)
type	Plugin type (bridge)
bridge	Bridge name (cni0)
isGateway	Acts as gateway
ipMasq	Enables NAT
ipam	Manages IP addresses
subnet	IP range
🔹 8. Example: What Actually Happens
👉 With above config:
1. Container is created
2. Bridge plugin is used
3. Container connects to cni0
4. Gets IP like: 10.22.0.5
5. Can talk to:
    * Other containers
    * Internet

🔹 9. Real-Life Analogy
* Container = 🏠 House
* CNI Plugin = 🛠 Road builder
* Bridge (cni0) = 🛣 Street
👉 When new container comes:
* Road (network) is built
* House connects to street
* Now communication is possible

🔹 10. Key Points for Exam / Interview
* CNI = networking standard in Kubernetes
* Runtime calls CNI plugin
* Plugins stored in /opt/cni/bin
* Config stored in /etc/cni/net.d
* First config file is selected
* Bridge plugin is most basic example
* IPAM handles IP allocation


📌 What this page is about
This page explains how networking works in Kubernetes services — basically, how different parts (pods and services) talk to each other inside and outside a cluster. 

🧠 Key Ideas
1. Pods vs Services
* Pods = where your app runs (they have their own IPs).
* Services = stable way to access pods (they give a fixed IP + DNS name).
* Instead of connecting directly to pods, you use services.
👉 Why? Because pod IPs can change, but services stay stable.

2. How Pod Networking Works
* Each pod gets:
    * Its own IP address
    * Its own network namespace
* Pods across different nodes can still talk using routes or overlay networks.

3. Types of Services
🔹 ClusterIP (default)
* Only works inside the cluster
* Used for internal apps like databases
* Example: backend services
🔹 NodePort
* Exposes service outside the cluster
* Opens a port on every node
* Used for web apps or external access

4. How Kubernetes Handles Traffic
* kubelet → creates pods and sets up networking
* kube-proxy → manages traffic routing
👉 Important:
* Services don’t have real network interfaces (they are virtual)
* kube-proxy creates rules to send traffic to the right pod

5. How Traffic Flows
1. Service gets an IP (like 10.x.x.x)
2. Pod sends request to service IP
3. kube-proxy redirects it to the actual pod
4. Uses iptables rules (default) to do this

6. IP Ranges
* Pods and services use different IP ranges
* They must NOT overlap

7. Example
* Pod IP → 10.244.1.2
* Service IP → 10.103.132.104
👉 Traffic to service IP is automatically forwarded to the pod.

🧾 Final Takeaway
* Services act like a stable gateway to pods
* kube-proxy ensures traffic reaches the correct pod
* ClusterIP = internal use
* NodePort = external access

📘 Summary: DNS in Kubernetes
This page explains how DNS works inside a Kubernetes cluster—basically how different parts (pods and services) find and talk to each other.

🔹 1. Kubernetes has its own DNS
* When you create a Kubernetes cluster, it automatically sets up a DNS server (CoreDNS).
* This DNS helps pods and services communicate using names instead of IP addresses.
👉 Think of it like:
* Instead of remembering phone numbers (IP), you use contact names (DNS).

🔹 2. Service DNS (Most Important)
* When you create a Service, Kubernetes automatically creates a DNS name for it.
* This DNS name maps to the service’s IP.
✅ Example
* Pod A (test pod): 10.244.1.5
* Pod B (web pod): 10.244.2.5
* Service: web-service → 10.107.37.188
Now instead of using IP:

curl http://web-service

✔️ This works because DNS maps web-service → service IP
👉 So pods talk using service names, not IPs.

🔹 3. Namespaces affect DNS names
* Kubernetes uses namespaces to organize resources.
* DNS names change depending on namespace.
✅ Examples
If service is in same namespace:

curl http://web-service

If service is in another namespace (e.g., apps):

curl http://web-service.apps

Full DNS name (FQDN):

curl http://web-service.apps.svc.cluster.local

🧠 Structure:

<service-name>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local

👉 Example:

web-service.apps.svc.cluster.local


🔹 4. Pod DNS (Optional feature)
* By default, pods do NOT get DNS names
* But if enabled, Kubernetes creates DNS using the pod IP
✅ Example
Pod IP:

10.244.2.5

Converted DNS:

10-244-2-5.apps.pod.cluster.local

Usage:

curl http://10-244-2-5.apps.pod.cluster.local

👉 Dots (.) in IP become dashes (-)

🔹 5. Key Takeaways
* Services always get DNS names ✅
* Pods usually don’t (unless enabled) ⚠️
* Use service names for communication (best practice)
* Namespaces change how DNS names look

🧩 Real-Life Analogy
* Pod = Person
* Service = Phone contact name
* IP address = Phone number

🔹 What is CoreDNS in Kubernetes?
CoreDNS is a DNS server inside Kubernetes that helps pods and services find and talk to each other using names instead of IP addresses. 
👉 Instead of remembering IPs like 10.244.2.5, you can use names like web-service.

🔹 Why do we need it?
* Pods are created and deleted frequently
* Their IP addresses keep changing
* Manually managing /etc/hosts is impossible at scale
👉 So Kubernetes uses CoreDNS to automatically manage names. 

🔹 How CoreDNS works
1. CoreDNS runs as a pod in kube-system namespace
2. Kubernetes creates a DNS service (usually kube-dns)
3. Every pod is configured to use this DNS server
Example inside a pod:

cat /etc/resolv.conf
nameserver 10.96.0.10

👉 This means: “Ask CoreDNS for any name resolution.”

🔹 Example: Service Communication
Suppose you have a service called web-service
You can access it in multiple ways:

curl http://web-service
curl http://web-service.default
curl http://web-service.default.svc.cluster.local

👉 All of these work because of DNS search settings. 

🔹 DNS Naming Structure
Full format:

<service-name>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local

Example:

web-service.default.svc.cluster.local

👉 This is called FQDN (Fully Qualified Domain Name)

🔹 Pod DNS (Important Difference)
* Services → easy to resolve with short names
* Pods → need full name
Example:

host 10-244-2-5.default.pod.cluster.local

👉 Pod IP is converted to hostname like:

10.244.2.5 → 10-244-2-5


🔹 CoreDNS Configuration (Corefile)
CoreDNS uses a config file called Corefile.
It does things like:
* Handle DNS queries
* Connect with Kubernetes API
* Forward unknown queries (e.g., google.com)
* Cache results
👉 Stored in a ConfigMap, so you can edit it easily.

🔹 Key Takeaways
* CoreDNS = central DNS system in Kubernetes
* Eliminates manual host file management
* Automatically updates DNS for pods & services
* Makes communication simple using names

🔹 Simple Real-Life Analogy
Think of CoreDNS like your phone contacts:
* Without it → You must remember phone numbers (IPs)
* With it → You just search names (service names)

🚀 Kubernetes Ingress — Simple Summary
🔹 What is Ingress?
Ingress is a smart router in Kubernetes that controls how external users access your apps.
👉 Instead of using multiple ports or load balancers, Ingress gives:
* One single entry point (IP/domain)
* Routes traffic based on URL path or domain
📌 Example:
* myapp.com/wear → wear-service
* myapp.com/watch → watch-service

🔹 Why not just use Services?
Before Ingress:
* You use NodePort / LoadBalancer
* Access like:http://<IP>:30000 ❌ (not user-friendly)
With Ingress:
* Access like:http://myapp.com/wear ✅
📌 Key problem solved:
* Too many load balancers = costly + complex 

🔹 Basic Architecture
￼
￼
￼
6
👉 Flow:User → Ingress → Service → Pod

🔹 Important Components
1. Ingress Controller
* Actual engine (like NGINX)
* Not installed by default ❗ 
Examples:
* NGINX
* HAProxy
* Traefik

2. Ingress Resource
* YAML file where you define rules

🔹 Simple Example (VS Code YAML)
✅ Single Service Routing

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: simple-ingress
spec:
  defaultBackend:
    service:
      name: wear-service
      port:
        number: 80

👉 All traffic → wear-service

✅ Path-Based Routing (MOST IMPORTANT)

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: path-ingress
spec:
  rules:
  - http:
      paths:
      - path: /wear
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: wear-service
            port:
              number: 80
      - path: /watch
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: watch-service
            port:
              number: 80

👉 Result:
* /wear → wear-service
* /watch → watch-service

🔹 Visual of Path Routing
￼
￼
￼
7

✅ Domain-Based Routing

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: domain-ingress
spec:
  rules:
  - host: myapp.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: main-service
            port:
              number: 80

👉 Different domains → different services

🔹 Key Points (Exam / Interview)
* Ingress = Layer 7 Load Balancer
* Works with:
    * HTTP / HTTPS
* Supports:
    * URL routing
    * Domain routing
    * SSL termination
* Needs:
    * Ingress Controller (mandatory)

🔹 Real-Life Example
Think of Ingress like:
🏢 Mall Security Gate
* One entrance
* Directs people to:
    * Food court 🍔
    * Movies 🎬
    * Shopping 🛍️

🔹 When to Use Ingress?
Use Ingress when:
* Multiple services
* Need clean URLs
* Want HTTPS + domain routing
* Want to reduce cloud costs

✅ Final One-Line Summary
👉 Ingress = Single entry point + smart routing for all your Kubernetes apps


🌐 Gateway API vs Ingress (Super Simple + Real Examples)
🧩 1. First understand the problem (Ingress)
👉 Imagine this situation:
You are building an online shopping website 🛒
You have:
* 👕 Clothes service → wear-service
* ⌚ Watches service → watch-service
You use Ingress to route traffic like this:
* wear.myshop.com → clothes
* watch.myshop.com → watches

❌ Problem with Ingress
👉 Both teams must edit the same Ingress file
Example:
* Team A (clothes) updates rules
* Team B (watches) also updates
💥 Result:
* Conflicts
* Mistakes
* Hard to manage
✔ This is called multi-team problem

❌ Another problem
Want advanced features?
Feature	Ingress Problem
HTTPS redirect	Needs annotations
CORS	Different for each controller
Traffic splitting	Complex
TCP/UDP	Not supported directly
👉 Example:
* NGINX config ≠ Traefik config
* You must learn different styles

🚀 2. Gateway API (The Better Way)
👉 Gateway API fixes all these problems.
Think of it like:
“Instead of one messy file → we divide work properly”

🏬 3. Real-life Example (Mall Analogy)
Imagine a shopping mall
👇 Roles:
Gateway API	Real life
GatewayClass	Mall builder
Gateway	Main entrance
HTTPRoute	Shop directions
🔧 4. How it works (with simple example)
🧱 Step 1: GatewayClass (Infrastructure)
👉 Defines which system is used
Example:
* Use NGINX or Envoy
💡 Simple meaning:
“What technology handles traffic?”

🚪 Step 2: Gateway (Entry point)
👉 Defines how users enter
Example:
* Port 80 → HTTP
* Port 443 → HTTPS
💡 Like:
“Main door of the mall”

🧭 Step 3: HTTPRoute (Routing rules)
👉 Defines where traffic goes

✅ Example:
User opens:

www.example.com/login

👉 HTTPRoute says:
* If path = /login
* Send to → login-service
✔ Done! 

🔐 5. HTTPS (TLS) Example
❌ Ingress way:
* Add TLS + annotations (messy)

✅ Gateway API way:
You clearly define:
* Port: 443
* Protocol: HTTPS
* Certificate: tls-secret
💡 Simple meaning:
“This door only allows secure traffic”

🚦 6. Traffic Splitting (Very Important)
👉 Used in real companies for safe deployment

🧪 Example:
You launch new version of app
* Old app → app-v1
* New app → app-v2

✅ Gateway API:

80% → app-v1
20% → app-v2

💡 Meaning:
Only few users test new version first
✔ Easy and built-in 

🌍 7. CORS Example (Frontend + Backend)
Problem:
Frontend (React app) calls backend API

❌ Ingress:
* Different config for NGINX, Traefik

✅ Gateway API:
You simply define:
* Allow origin: *
* Allow methods: GET, POST
💡 Meaning:
“Anyone can call this API safely”
✔ Same config works everywhere

🧠 8. Why Gateway API is better
✅ Simple reasons:
✔ Teams don’t fight over same file✔ No controller-specific configs✔ Supports more protocols (HTTP, TCP, gRPC)✔ Built-in advanced features

⚡ 9. Final Understanding (Super Easy)
👉 Ingress = Old system
* One file
* Messy
* Hard to scale

👉 Gateway API = New system
* Clean structure
* Team-friendly
* Powerful

🎯 10. One-line summary
Gateway API = “Divide work + simplify traffic management in Kubernetes”

🔁 Quick Example Recap
Scenario:
User visits:

www.shop.com/api

👉 Flow:
1. Gateway receives request
2. HTTPRoute checks path /api
3. Sends request to → api-service
✔ Clean and simple


